(KPI: 10Y yield / beta)\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response\nIf Tyler successfully dominates (SaaS + AI + transactions), competitors (OpenGov,\nOracle, Microsoft) will respond with lower-priced AI-native modules. Impact: \u22121.5\n(mild negative). If Tyler fails, rivals take mid-market share. The public-sector\nmarket tolerates 2\u20133 vendors \u2014 a price war is less likely than quiet share drift.\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity\nPositive loop: stock price rebound \u2192 SBC attracts talent, M&A currency for FTR-type\ndeals. Negative loop (current): price down 39% \u2192 buyback value trap narrative,\npressure to use debt for buybacks, potential talent retention risk. Net: \u22120.5.\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior evolution\nEarly adopters (counties) already migrated; mainstream now migrating; late adopters\n(small towns) price-sensitive. LTV/CAC may compress at the margin. Retention has been\nstrong; watch for any churn uptick >5% of ARR.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario probabilities (sum = 100%)\n\u2022 BEAR (20%): Public-sector budget squeeze + AI disruption + continued de-rating.\n  Rev CAGR FY27\u201330 = 4%; margin flat 27%; SBC 7% of rev; no net buyback.\n  IV = $235/sh [Estimate].\n\u2022 BASE (55%): Management executes 2030 plan within our conservative envelope.\n  Rev CAGR ~7.4%; margin to 29.2%; buyback \u22122.5%/yr. IV = $348/sh [Estimate].\n\u2022 BULL (25%): SaaS + transactions reaccelerate; AI/transactions become a new\n  revenue engine; revenue CAGR ~9\u201310% to $3.6B by 2030; EBITDA margin 31%;\n  EPS CAGR ~15%; exit 25x. IV = $440\u2013450/sh [Estimate].\nExpected value = 0.20\u00d7235 + 0.55\u00d7348 + 0.25\u00d7445 = $350/sh.\n50% CI: $290\u2013$430 | 80% CI: $235\u2013$520 [Assumption \u2014 scenario distribution].\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian update\nPrior (Section 1.5): P(\u2265base success) = 70%.\nEvidence: + (record SaaS bookings, raised guidance, raised 2030 targets) | \u2212 (ARR\ngrowth 8.2%, short interest rising, stock \u221239%).\nLikelihood ratio \u2248 1.1 \u2192 Posterior P(\u2265base) \u2248 75\u201380%; use 78%.\n\nStep 4-4. Expected price & MoS\nExpected price = $350 (EV). MoS = (348 \u2212 350.73)/348 = \u22120.8% \u2192 NONE.\n\nStep 4-5. Margin of safety thresholds\nAt $300: MoS 14% (adequate \u2192 buy zone). At $270: MoS 23% (strong).\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: current price embeds ~12% EPS CAGR through FY2030 with a 25x\nexit multiple \u2014 i.e., a 4\u20135 year outlook. My horizon: 5 years. Spread \u2248 0.\nNo meaningful horizon arbitrage. A 3-year horizon would justify only ~$295 (at 22x\nFY29E EPS of $16.40), implying 16% downside if you demand a 5-year payback. This raises\nthe bar on entry price.\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nConviction score (15 max):\n1. Business quality: 2.5/3 (wide moat, Excellent cash, Adequate ROIC)\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 1/3 (fair value, 0% MoS)\n3. Macro alignment: 1.5/3 (neutral/mild headwind)\n4. Thesis clarity: 2.5/3 (KPIs measurable: ARR, adj. EBITDA margin, FCF margin, SBC)\n5. Execution traceability: 2/3 (guidance raised; FTR/converts untested)\nTotal: 9.5/15 \u2192 MEDIUM. After Red-Team gap (Section 9): downgrade to 7.5/15 \u2192\nLOW-MEDIUM.\nConviction decay: \u22121 every 6 months absent confirming evidence (auto-applied next review).\nSize max: 3\u20134% portfolio weight (half of even the Low-Medium band, because MoS \u2248 0).\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Short interest: 9.94% of float (3.50M shares), DTC 4.37, MoM +2.5 pts [Actual \u2014\n  pack, 2026-08-14]. Elevated vs S&P median (~3\u20135%): meaningful bearish crowding.\n  Squeeze potential exists, but bears have been right (stock \u221239% from high).\n\u2022 Institutional ownership: 107.3% [Aggregator \u2014 distorted by double-counting; true\n  passive+active ~85\u201395%]. Index-heavy holder base \u2192 ETF-flow vulnerability.\n\u2022 Insider: 0.52% ownership [Actual]. No cluster buying. [Unconfirmed insider activity;\n  secondary sources mention insider selling as a caution signal \u2014 unverified.]\n\u2022 Options: not in pack [Unconfirmed]; the \u221239% drawdown + rising short interest suggests\n  put-heavy flow.\n\u2022 Sentiment: Sell-side mean target $426.64 / median $427.50 [Aggregator] \u2014 street\n  remains 21\u201322% above the market price; this is a BULLISH-analyst/bearish-tape\n  divergence. Typical of a mid-de-rating, not a capitulation.\n\u2022 Crowding Risk Score: 7/15 \u2192 apply \u22120.5pct position-size adjustment (above).\n\u2022 Sentiment conclusion: no contrarian buy signal; wait for either a price flush to\n  <$300 or a fundamental turnaround in ARR growth.\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nNo other holdings given; judgments are marginal/analytical.\n\u2022 Low beta 0.81 [Actual] \u2192 portfolio diversifier vs high-octane software names.\n\u2022 Negative correlation to rates: hurts when 10Y rises (2022-style, 2026-style).\n\u2022 Positive correlation to government spending; tailwind if fiscal expansion occurs.\n\u2022 Functions as a \"bond proxy plus growth.\" In a 60/40, TYL behaves like 0.4 bonds /\n  0.6 equities in a rate shock, but like 0.2 bonds / 0.8 equities in a credit shock.\n\u2022 Position sizing: max 3% (Low-Medium conviction, 0% MoS, crowding \u22120.5pct).\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 P(win) = P(base + bull) = 80% | P(loss) = 20%.\n\u2022 Mean win = (445\u2212350.73) = +$94 | Mean loss = (350.73\u2212235) = \u2212$116.\n\u2022 Edge = 0.80\u00d794 \u2212 0.20\u00d7116 = 75.2 \u2212 23.2 = +$52 per share vs 0.8/0.2 odds...\n  \u2014 but the BASE case is priced to deliver only the required return (alpha \u2248 0).\n  Kelly on alpha only: base alpha = +0.4% (\u22480); bull alpha = +27%; bear alpha = \u221233%.\n  Expected alpha = 0.55\u00d70 + 0.25\u00d70.27 + 0.20\u00d7(\u22120.33) = 0.0675 \u2212 0.066 = +0.15% \u2192 \u22480.\n\u2022 Full Kelly \u2248 0\u20131% of capital. Fractional (\u00bc) Kelly = 0\u20130.25%.\n\u2022 Layer 5 (3%) and Layer 6.5 (0\u20131%) differ by ~2\u20133pct, within the 20pct rule because\n  absolute size is single digits; resolution: use the smaller (0\u20131%) for new money and\n  3% only as a maximum if price drops to $300.\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nRisks\n1. Public-sector budget cyclicality (property tax, sales tax, federal grants) \u2014 medium-high.\n2. Rising 10Y (4.71%) continuing to compress software multiples \u2014 medium.\n3. Competitive/AI disruption (Oracle, Microsoft, OpenGov, Granicus) \u2014 medium.\n4. SBC dilution: $151M FY25 \u2192 ~$165M FY26 \u2248 47% of GAAP NI [Actual/Estimate] \u2014 high\n   (real but partially offset by buybacks).\n5. Convertible debt ($1.4B, 0.50% due 2031) \u2014 conversion/dilution overhang if stock\n   exceeds $405.94; capped call at $655.77 \u2014 low-medium.\n6. Cybersecurity/ransomware litigation (existing lawsuit) \u2014 medium, recurring tail.\n7. M&A integration (For The Record) \u2014 low-medium.\nCatalysts\n1. Quarterly SaaS bookings/ARR reacceleration (>9%).\n2. Quantification of raised 2030 targets (Investor Day follow-through) [Unconfirmed].\n3. $1.745B remaining buyback execution.\n4. AI transactions platform scaling (record transaction-based wins per Q2'26 PR).\n5. Fed pivot / 10Y <4% \u2192 multiple re-rating.\n\nPre-mortem (failure scenarios)\n1. \"Budget crisis 2028\": property/muni tax collapse \u2192 ARR growth <3%.\n   Early warning: Tyler guidance cuts, local-tax-receipt data, transactions volume negative.\n2. \"AI disruption\": an AI-native suite undercuts the implementation-heavy model.\n   Early warning: win-rate deterioration, churn >5% of ARR, implementation backlog shrink.\n3. \"Capital-structure spiral\": debt-funded buybacks + forced convert conversion in a\n   weak tape \u2192 leverage >3x EBITDA, rating downgrade.\n   Early warning: net debt/EBITDA >2.0x, stock >$406 for a sustained period, S&P/Moody's\n   actions.\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Common bear trigger with the rest of a typical portfolio: interest-rate rises\n  (beta 0.81, but software de-rates harder; 2022 saw \u221240%+ in SaaS).\n\u2022 Tail (correlation=1) CVaR: In a 2022-style stress (+150bps rates, \u221220% market),\n  TYL could lose ~20\u201325% from here \u2192 ~$270\u2013280. In a 2008-style crisis, losses\n  would be lower (~15%) but correlated with credit stress. In a 2020-style liquidity\n  crisis, ~20\u201325% drawdown with fast recovery.\n\u2022 5% CVaR estimate at 12-month horizon: \u2248 \u221220% (to ~$280).\n\u2022 Real-asset hedge: none. Dividend: none. This is a long-duration rate-sensitive asset.\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nThesis KPIs (from Tornado top-3):\n1. ARR growth (quarterly): hold above 7%. At <6% \u2192 thesis breaks \u2192 re-evaluate.\n2. Adj. EBITDA margin (quarterly): hold above 26% (FY26 guide implies ~27.5\u201328%).\n   At <24.5% \u2192 break.\n3. FCF margin (company definition): hold 26\u201328% [guidance]. At <22% \u2192 break.\n4. SBC/revenue: hold <7.5%. At >9% \u2192 break (dilution out of control).\n5. Net debt/adj. EBITDA: hold <2.5x. At >3x \u2192 break.\n6. Buyback execution: 5.6% YTD; expect 8\u201310% by year-end. No buyback for 2 consecutive\n   quarters \u2192 negative signal.\n7. Short interest: if >12% of float, monitor for squeeze; if >15%, treat as crowded.\n8. Data events: any additional breach headlines \u2192 cut thesis by 2 points.\nRe-evaluation triggers: ARR <5%, churn >6%, margin <24%, CFO departure, SEC/DOJ\ninvestigation escalation.\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nShort thesis (7 bullets, same data):\n1. GAAP ROIC \u22488.5% (\u2248cost of capital) \u2014 the \"quality compounder\" narrative is a\n   non-GAAP artifact. SBC ($151M in 2025) = 47% of GAAP NI.\n2. ARR growth is 8.2% and decelerating; transaction growth (35% of revenue) is 3.5%.\n   The \"SaaS growth 21.7%\" is a mix-shift illusion \u2014 total recurring revenue grew 8.2%.\n3. At 26.8x FY26 non-GAAP EPS, the price embeds ~12% EPS CAGR for 4\u20135 years. A single\n   guidance miss resets the multiple to 20x \u2192 $270.\n4. The company borrowed $1.4B (0.50% convert) partly to buy back stock at $312\u2013350.\n   Management is financially engineering EPS growth; if the business stalls, the\n   convert overhang (conversion at $405.94, 3.45M shares) hits per-share value.\n5. Public-sector budget risk is underappreciated: high rates raise pension/property-\n   cost burdens; software is getting cut in 2026 before headcount.\n6. AI is a two-edged sword: if a rival ships an AI-native ERP at 50% lower cost,\n   Tyler's 12\u201324 month implementation model is competitively exposed.\n7. The 39% drawdown already happened \u2014 but from a 44x GAAP P/E peak. Mean-reversion\n   to 20x forward non-GAAP EPS implies $260, not $350.\n\nLogic combat:\n\u2022 Long thesis strength: 8/10 (moat, recurring revenue, FCF, buybacks; but ROIC weak)\n\u2022 Short thesis strength: 7/10 (SBC, valuation, deceleration, balance-sheet\n  engineering)\n\u2022 Gap = 1 \u2192 conviction downgraded (applied in Section 5).\n\nAdversarial data: Kaskela Law shareholder investigation [Unconfirmed \u2014 web brief],\nransomware class-action lawsuit [Unconfirmed \u2014 web brief]. Both unverified in primary\nsources; treat as claims that increase legal tail risk.\n\nKilled arguments (and the conditions that would revive them):\n\u2022 \"M&A creates value\": killed until FTR shows ROIC>WACC; revive if FTR revenue grows\n  >20% in FY27.\n\u2022 \"AI will re-rate the stock\": killed at current price action; revive if AI bookings\n  move ARR growth >12%.\n\u2022 \"Cheap on cash flow\": killed because owner-FCF (after SBC) yield is 3.4%; revive if\n  price <$300.\n\u2022 \"Short squeeze imminent\": killed by 4.37 DTC (not extreme); revive if DTC >10.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nA. Methodology audit: Engine 1 (Stable/Quality) selected with justification; no\n   DCF-only conclusion; no peer-only; no TAM-only. PASS.\nB. Valuation audit: DCF (FCFE), reverse DCF, multiples, scenario EV and P/E exit\n   all converged to $330\u2013365. PASS.\nC. Bias audit: SBC expensed; GAAP vs non-GAAP separated; buyback share-count\n   reductions verified against pack share counts; convertible size discrepancy\n   flagged, not hidden. PASS.\nD. Consistency audit: Did not mix trailing GAAP with forward non-GAAP; did not\n   use vendor total debt (stale) without reconciling;