25\n[Estimate]; 50% CI $330\u2013$500; 80% CI $230\u2013$545. Scenario-weighted expected\nvalue $406 [Estimate]. 17.5% margin of safety in base case. Conviction\n11.5/15 [High]. Target position 5% of portfolio, entered in tranches.\n\nKey expectation gap: the market is discounting Tyler's cash flows at ~9.0\u20139.4%\n[Estimate\u2014reverse DCF], while the beta-implied WACC is ~8.0% [Arithmetic].\nThe gap is the entire thesis; it can close either by price appreciation or by\na higher-for-longer rate path. Monitoring rules are calibrated to that risk.\n\nData integrity notes:\n- I used the filed TTM and fiscal-year series as the highest authority; vendor\n  restatements are cross-checks only.\n- I detected that the FY2025 \"working capital release\" of $287M is not\n  operational: it reflects reclassification of the $600M 2026 convertible debt\n  into current liabilities [Estimate\u2014consistency check with Q1 2026 filing\n  (company repaid the $600M convertible in April 2026) and FY2025 balance-sheet\n  step: CL +$689M vs CA +$403M]. I therefore did NOT subtract $287M from OCF\n  when normalizing FCF. This is the single most important pack-data trap.\n- The FY2025 share count (43.8M diluted) is stale; current shares outstanding\n  are 40.93M due to the 2026 buyback [Actual\u2014pack SECTION 2].\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1-1. Macro variable check (as of August 2026):\n\n 10-Year Treasury yield ......... ~4.5% [Unconfirmed \u2014 I could not retrieve an\n                                      exact August 2026 closing level; based on\n                                      the following context I bound it at\n                                      4.3\u20134.8%]. Context: CPI y/y 3.4% [Actual\u2014\n                                      Atlanta Fed source], PCE 3.7% [Actual],\n                                      PPI 4.7% [Actual], Fed funds futures\n                                      implying 60.6% probability of a HIKE at\n                                      the 9/16/26 FOMC [Actual\u2014market pricing].\n Fed policy stance .............. Tightening bias (market pricing net hike)\n DXY ............................ Not retrieved [Unconfirmed]; elevated-rate\n                                      environment implies firm USD\n GDP ............................ +1.5% Q2 2026 vs +2.1% Q1 2026 [Actual\u2014US\n                                      GDP]; decelerating\n Inflation ...................... 3.4\u20133.7% core measures [Actual]; ABOVE\n                                      target, sticky\n Unemployment ................... 4.1% [Actual]\n Credit spreads ................. Not retrieved [Unconfirmed]; equity selloff\n                                      implies modest widening\n Global liquidity ............... Tightening; QT continuing\n\n1-2. Regime probability distribution (must sum to 100%):\n - Stagflation (High rates + Low growth) ....... 45%\n - Reflation (High rates + Growth) ............. 25%\n - Recession (Low rates + Low growth) .......... 15%\n - Goldilocks (Low rates + Growth) ............. 15%\n TOTAL ....................................... 100%\n   [All estimates: based on the macro data above]\n\n1-3. Tyler macro sensitivity (5 axes, \u22123 to +3):\n - Interest rate sensitivity ......... \u22122   (long-duration multiple; 87%\n                                              recurring revenue)\n - Dollar sensitivity ................ +1   (~90% domestic; strong dollar is\n                                              neutral-to-positive vs US-focused)\n - Liquidity sensitivity ............. +1   (net-cash balance sheet, buybacks)\n - Economic cycle sensitivity ........ +2   (DEFENSIVE: courts, property tax,\n                                              public safety are mandatory budgets)\n - Commodity sensitivity ............. +1   (no direct exposure)\n1-4. Macro alignment: NET NEUTRAL. The macro mix mildly favors Tyler's\ndefensive demand profile (stagflation 45%), but the high-rate path compresses\nits multiple. No conviction adjustment from macro (per framework rules).\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1.5-1. Base rates relevant to Tyler [Estimate \u2014 based on my training knowledge\nof long-run industry studies and the pack's own data; I could not retrieve a\nfresh database at this moment]:\n - 5-year survival of an established, profitable, 87%-recurring software\n   company with a 30+ year installed base: > 95%\n - Sustained (>8%) organic growth for 5 years from a $2.5B revenue base:\n   ~ 35\u201350% (most large-cap software decelerates to mid-single digits)\n - Platform transition (legacy to SaaS) fully executed: Tyler has already\n   passed the riskiest phase; success base rate now ~ 85%\n - Small bolt-on M&A (FTR, $212.5M) achieving ROIC > WACC within 5y:\n   ~ 50%\n - Large multi-year margin expansion target (30% \u2192 mid-30s non-GAAP) being\n   achieved: ~ 40\u201355%\n\n1.5-2. Deviation of my analysis from base rate: My base+bull probability\n(75%) is above the ~45% base rate for sustained high-single-digit growth.\nThree company-specific justifications:\n (1) Tyler's revenue is 87% recurring [Arithmetic: ~$560M recurring / $645M\n     revenue in Q2 2026 \u2014 company IR release]; contractual multi-year\n     arrangements with state/local governments make revenue mechanically\n     sticky.\n (2) Demand is budget-mandatory (courts, property tax administration, public\n     safety, 911; not discretionary IT).\n (3) Prior evidence: FY2019\u2013FY2025 revenue CAGR was 13.6% [Arithmetic] even\n     THROUGH the pandemic years, demonstrating budget resilience.\n Therefore I keep the deviation but MODERATE growth to 7.5\u20139.0% in base\n (not the company's 10% target). This is the disciplined position.\n\n1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: My bull+base probability (75%) vs base rate\n(~45%) is 1.67x \u2014 below the 2.0x red flag; above 1.5x, so I required the\nthree justifications above and I moderated the growth path. Pass.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n2-1. Business understanding (three sentences):\n Tyler Technologies sells mission-critical enterprise software to US\nstate/local government: ERP/finance, courts & justice, public safety,\nproperty tax, permitting, and digital government/payments. Revenue is now\n~87% recurring, split between SaaS subscriptions and transaction-based\npayments processing. Customers face 12\u201324-month implementations and\neffectively never leave.\n\nUnderstanding rating: HIGH\n\n2-2. Moat assessment and direction:\n - Switching costs ............ 3/3  (12\u201324-month implementation; data\n                                      conversion disutility)\n - Intangible assets .......... 2/3  (long-standing public-sector brand,\n                                      product IP)\n - Network effects ............ 1/3  (limited; court-ecosystem data improves\n                                      with FTR acquisition)\n - Cost advantage ............. 1/3  (NOT cost-competitive vs Oracle/Microsoft)\n - Economies of scale ......... 2/3  ($245M R&D amortized over 5,000+ systems;\n                                      cloud platform adds operating leverage)\n - Regulatory barriers ........ 2/3  (CJIS compliance, procurement\n                                      certifications, public-record mandates)\n - Data moat .................. 2/3  (proprietary parcel data, court records,\n                                      workflow data)\n TOTAL: 13/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT (range 11\u201315). Direction: STABLE, slightly\n widening in the courts/legal vertical (For The Record acquisition creates a\n unified courtroom data ecosystem [Company\u2014LinkedIn/IR via secondary source]).\n Narrowing risk: price-sensitive mid-market counties face cheaper alternatives\n [Estimate \u2014 research brief, unverified].\n\n2-3. Lynch classification: STALWART (growing ~8\u201310%, high margins, stable\nbalance sheet, defensive end-market). Core checkpoints for a stalwart:\n - [PASS] predictably growing EPS \u2014 non-GAAP EPS guidance raised to\n   $12.95\u201313.20 FY2026 [Company guidance \u2014 Zacks 2026-07-30]\n - [PASS] low cyclicality \u2014 public-sector budgets\n - [PASS] moderate growth: total revenue +8.2% YoY Q2 2026 [Filed]\n - [WATCH] margins: GAAP op margin 15.3% [Arithmetic]; non-GAAP margin\n   expansion is the value bridge\n\n2-4. Financial strength scorecard:\n - Revenue 3-yr CAGR (FY22\u2013FY25): 13.6% [Arithmetic]\n - Revenue growth, last 4 quarters (Q3'25\u2013Q2'26): +8.2% [Arithmetic]\n - Operating margin: 15.3% TTM; 12-yr median 15.4% [Arithmetic]\n - EPS growth: FY2025 net income $316M vs FY2024 $263M = +20.2% [Arithmetic]\n - ROE: 9.7% [Aggregator]; GAAP ROIC: ~8.8% [Arithmetic \u2014 NOPAT $286M /\n   IC $3.25B]\n - FCF margin: 29.4% (company definition); owner FCF margin 22.8%\n   [Arithmetic \u2014 OCF $731M \u2212 capex $16M \u2212 SBC ~$155M / $2.430B]\n - Debt: $1,437.5M convertible (0.50% due 2031) + ~$43M op leases vs cash +\n   ST investments ~$1.0B \u2192 NET DEBT ~+$450M [Estimate \u2014 balance-sheet\n   composition post-May 2026 [Unconfirmed], but sign and magnitude robust]\n - Interest coverage: very high; coupon on the convertible is 0.50%\n   [Actual \u2014 BusinessWire pricing release].\n Judgment: EXCELLENT stability, GOOD returns. Not exceptional GAAP ROIC.\n\n2-5. Capital allocation scorecard:\n 1. Buyback efficiency ........ 3/3  (2.5% of shares YTD through April 2026 at\n                                     $350\u2013$450 vs intrinsic $425+; new $1.5B\n                                     authorization July 2026 [Company\u2014press])\n 2. M&A track record .......... 2/3  (FTR $212.5M \u2014 small, strategic,\n                                     bolt-on; no major impairments visible in\n                                     pack)\n 3. Capex efficiency .......... 3/3  (asset-light: capex/OCF = 2.2%\n                                     [Filed]; incremental ROIC high)\n 4. Rationality of mix ........ 3/3  (R&D ~$245M FY2026 [Company guidance] +\n                                     tuck-in M&A + buybacks; balanced)\n 5. Guidance accuracy ......... 2/3  (raised FY26 EPS above consensus at Q2;\n                                     revenue slightly missed Q2 [$645M vs\n                                     ~$648M consensus] [Second-hand\u2014Motley\n                                     Fool])\n TOTAL: 13/15 [Good-to-Excellent].\n Insider ownership 0.52% [Aggregator] \u2192 no bonus; 6-mo insider activity:\n [Unconfirmed \u2014 no data] \u2192 no adjustment.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n2.5-1. R&D capitalization (3-yr amortization):\n R&D estimates: FY2024 ~$210M, FY2025 ~$230M, FY2026E $245M [Assumption;\n FY2026 is Company guidance, earlier years are interpolations around the\n $245M guidance and revenue scale]\n Adjusted OI = GAAP OI $358M + R&D $230M \u2212 amortization ~$220M = ~$368M\n [Estimate]\n Unamortized R&D asset \u2248 ~$330M [Estimate]\n2.5-2. SBC proper treatment:\n Owner FCF = FCF \u2212 SBC = $638M \u2212 $151M = $487M FY2025 [Arithmetic]\n Per-share dilution: 1-yr net issuance +0.7%, 3-yr +2.0% CAGR [Arithmetic\u2014\n pack SECTION 7]; 2026 buybacks fully offset dilution [Estimate]\n2.5-3. Operating leases capitalized: $43M FY2025 [Filed] \u2192 adjust net debt.\n2.5-4. Brand/data asset: [Assumption] +$200M to IC (public-sector brand +\n proprietary data).\n2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC = adjusted NOPAT (~$306M) / (IC $3.25B + unamortized\n R&D $330M + brand $200M \u2248 $3.78B) = ~8.1% [Estimate].\n GAAP ROIC ~8.8% [Arithmetic] \u2014 the gap is small and NEGATIVE direction:\n intangibles adjustment reduces reported ROIC because Tyler already expenses\n R&D and its balance-sheet capital is low. This is typical of an\n asset-light company. Conclusion: Tyler earns ~8\u20139% ROIC on conservative\n adjusted capital \u2014 around its WACC, not a wide-moat ROIC. This tempers the\n quality narrative: value must come from growth + FCF generation, not from a\n high ROIC moat.\n2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett) FY2025:\n NI $316M + D&A $138M \u2212 maintenance capex $40M [Assumption\u2014includes\n capitalized software ~$18M] \u2212 \u0394WC (normalized to ~0; see Section 0 data\n integrity note) = ~$414M [Estimate].\n TTM owner FCF (OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC): ~$560M [Arithmetic] \u2014 the DCF below uses\n this line.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3-1. Discount rate derivation:\n Risk-free (10Y): 4.5% [Estimate; [Unconfirmed] actual August 2026 level \u2014\n  bounded 4.3\u20134.8% by macro context (CPI 3.4%, market pricing Fed HIKE)]\n Beta: 0.809 [Aggregator\u2014vendor window unknown]\n Equity risk premium: 4.75% [Assumption]\n Cost of equity: 4.5% + 0.809 \u00d7 4.75% = 8.34% [Arithmetic]\n Cost of debt, after-tax: 2.0% [Assumption \u2014 0.50% convertible + a small\n  revolver; after-tax ~0.4\u20132.0%]\n Market equity: $14.356B; gross debt: ~$1.48B (converts + op leases)\n  [Arithmetic]; weights: 90.7% / 9.3%\n WACC = 8.34% \u00d7 0.907 + 2.0% \u00d7 0.093 = 7.75% \u2192 ROUNDED TO 8.0%\n  [Arithmetic + Assumption]\n\n3-2. Explicit forecast (see source notes after the table):\n\n| Year | Revenue $B [tag] | Growth [tag] | Owner FCF margin [tag] | Owner FCF $M [Arith] | PV@8% $M |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | $2.555 | +9.6% | 21.5% | $549 | $509 |\n| 2027 | $2.785 | +9.0% | 22.3% | $621 | $533 |\n| 2028 | $3.022 | +8.5% | 23.0% | $695 | $552 |\n| 2029 | $3.263 | +8.0% | 23.8% | $777 | $571 |\n| 2030 | $3.508 | +7.5% | 24.5% | $859 | $585 |\n| 2031 | $3.754 | +7.0% | 25.0% | $939 | $592 |\n| 2032 | $3.979 | +6.0% | 25.5% | $1,015 | $592 |\n| 2033 | $4.178 | +5.0% | 26.0% | $1,086 | $587 |\n| Terminal | g = 3.5% | | | TV: $24,985 | $13,499 |\n| TOTAL PV of explicit FCF | | | | | $4,519 |\n| EV | | | | | $18,018 |\n\nForecast-driver sourcing:\n - FY2026 revenue: $2.535\u20132.575B midpoint $2.555B [Company guidance \u2014 Zacks,\n   2026-07-30]\n - FY2027\u20132033 revenue growth: my ramp. It is BELOW the company's 2030\n   \"double-digit recurring revenue growth, 10% top-line\" vision [Company\n   guidance \u2014 Morningstar 6/15/26 + secondary source], because the recent\n   reported trend is decelerating: Q4'25 +6.3%, Q1'26 +8.6%, Q2'26 +8.2%\n   [Filed]. The post-guidance period is my assumption, informed by that\n   deceleration and by base-rate evidence (\u00a71.5-1). [Assumption for 2027+]\n - Owner FCF margins: the path starts from FY2025 actual owner FCF margin\n   of 20.9% [Arithmetic: 487/2332], is consistent with company-guided\n   non-GAAP FCF margin of 26\u201328% for FY2026 [Company guidance\u2014Zacks] minus\n   SBC ~6% of revenue, and drifts toward \"low-30s non-GAAP\" by 2030 [Company\n   guidance\u2014Investor Day June 2026] minus an assumed ~6% SBC drag.\n   [Assumption for the drift; company guidance anchors the endpoints]\n - Capital expenditure: filed PP&E capex is tiny ($16M FY2025; $16M TTM)\n   [Filed]; capitalized software ~$18M on top of that [Company guidance \u2014\n   ainv.com secondary source]. Total cash capex \u2248 1.4% of revenue\n   [Arithmetic+Assumption], embedded in the owner FCF margin.\n - Terminal growth of 3.5% [Assumption \u2014 long-run nominal GDP; consistent\n   with a 26%-owner-FCF-margin, sticky-revenue terminal business].\n Terminal value formula: FCF2033 \u00d7 1.035 / (0.08 \u2212 0.035).\n\n3-3. Equity bridge and per-share value:\n EV: $18,018M\n Less net debt: \u2212$450M [Estimate \u2014 $1,437.5M converts + $43M leases \u2212 ~$1.0B\n cash+ST investments; composition post-Q2'26 partly [Unconfirmed]]\n Equity value: $17,568M\n Diluted shares: 41.0M [Arithmetic \u2014 40.93M outstanding [Actual] + ~0.5\u20131.0M\n net option/RSU overhang [Assumption]; convertible is out-of-the-money at\n $351 and treated as debt; if stock > ~$550, add ~2.4M shares [Estimate]]\n INTRINSIC VALUE (base): $425\u2013428 \u2192 I use $425 [Estimate]\n Margin of safety vs T0 $350.73: 17.5% \u2192 \"ADEQUATE\" band (15\u201330%)\n 50% CI: $330\u2013$500 [Estimate]\n 80% CI: $230\u2013$545 (from scenario distribution, \u00a74) [Estimate]\n\n3-4. Reverse DCF (what the market implies):\n At T0 EV $14.806B ($14.356B market cap + $450M net debt [Estimate]), given\n my explicit forecast PV of $4.52B, the market's implied terminal value is\n ~$10.3B PV \u2192 terminal value ~$19.0B at 2033. With FCF2033 of $1,086M and\n g=3.5%, this implies a discount rate of ~9.4% [Arithmetic]. ALTERNATIVELY,\n at an 8% WACC, the market is implying terminal growth of ~2.0% [Arithmetic],\n below my 3.5% and below long-run nominal GDP. EITHER interpretation means\n the market is embedding materially more pessimism than my base.\n\n3-5. Sensitivity \u2014 intrinsic value per share to WACC \u00d7 terminal growth:\n\n| WACC TG | 7.5% | 8.0% | 8.5% | 9.0% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3.0% | $443 | $407 | $356 | $324 |\n| 3.5% | $486 | $425 | $382 | $330 |\n| 4.0% | ~$540 | ~$450 | ~$400 | ~$345 |\n\n3-6. Multiples cross-check (no multiple-only conclusion):\n - Forward non-GAAP P/E: $350.73 / $13.08 = 26.8x [Arithmetic \u2014 company\n   guidance midpoint $12.95\u201313.20]\n - Trailing GAAP P/E: 41.7x [Aggregator] \u2014 note the large SBC/add-back wedge\n - EV/Sales (forward): $14.806B / $2.555B = 5.8x [Arithmetic]\n - PEG (forward, consensus EPS growth 17.2% [Aggregator]): 1.56x [Arithmetic]\n - Owner FCF yield (TTM): $560M / $14.356B = 3.9% [Arithmetic]\n - Each of these sits at the LOW END of Tyler's 5\u201310-year historical range;\n   the first two are consistent with a $400\u2013460 fair value at historical\n   average multiples [Estimate]. Cross-check PASSES \u2014 it does not contradict\n   the DCF.\n\n3-7. Tornado (top-3 value drivers, \u00b11\u03c3):\n 1. WACC (range 7.5\u20139.0): $345\u2013$486 \u2192 sensitivity \u00b117% around base\n 2. Terminal growth (3.0\u20134.0): $407\u2013$450 \u2192 \u00b15%\n 3. FY2027\u201330 revenue growth (7.0\u201310.0): ~$390\u2013$510 \u2192 \u00b114%\n Top 3 KPIs are therefore: macro rates (WACC), recurring revenue growth\n trajectory, and SaaS/transaction growth mix.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3.5-1. Competitive response: IF Tyler executes well (mid-30s op margin by\n2030), competitors will respond: Oracle/Microsoft can bundle public-sector\nofferings with cloud credits; regional price-cutters can undercut in the\nmid-market (Tyler is 30\u201360% more expensive than alternatives [Estimate\u2014\nresearch brief]). Counter-move impact on Tyler: \u22121 to \u22122 over a 5-year\nhorizon; partially offset by Tyler's superior public-safety/compliance\ncertifications and implementation depth. [Assumption/Estimate]\n\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: POSITIVE loop already broke: the \u221239% stock decline\nremoved M&A currency and slightly weakens SBC-based talent attraction;\nNEGATIVE loop is muted (customers do not leave over stock price). At this\nlevel, buybacks become value-accretive (self-correcting loop). [Estimate]\n\n3.5-3. Customer evolution: Early adopter phase (large counties converting to\nSaaS) is largely done; Tyler is now selling to mid-market and small\ncounties \u2192 smaller initial deal sizes, longer sales cycles, higher price\nsensitivity. LTV/CAC economics are still positive [Estimate\u2014based on 30\u201340\nyear install base and ~87% recurring revenue], but the attach rate per new\nsystem is lower than in the early SaaS years. This is already embedded in my\nbase-case growth deceleration.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (Bayesian probabilities)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n4-1/4-2. Scenario definitions and probabilities (sum = 100%):\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Revenue 2030 | Owner FCF margin 2033 | WACC | TG | IV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BEAR | 25% | $3.05B (+3\u20136% CAGR) | 20% | 9.0% | 3.0% | $230 |\n| BASE | 50% | $3.51B (+7.5\u20139% CAGR) | 26% | 8.0% | 3.5% | $425 |\n| BULL | 25% | $3.74B (+10% CAGR) | 30.5% | 8.0% | 3.5% | $545 |\n\n Prior (\u00a71.5): P(sustained >8% growth) = ~45%.\n Update triggers observed: Q2'26 SaaS revenue +21.7%, recurring revenue\n ~87% of total [Company IR\u2014Zacks snippet]; FY26 EPS guidance raised above\n consensus [Company guidance]; GAAP net income +10.5% YoY in Q2'26 [Filed];\n transaction revenue only +3.5% YoY [Company IR\u2014weak signal]; 10Y elevated\n [Actual macro context]; software sector derating on AI-budget-cannibalization\n fear [Second-hand\u2014Motley Fool 7/30/26].\n Posterior: bear 25% / base 50% / bull 25% \u2192 P(favorable) = 75%. This is a\n 1.67x multiplier on the base rate, justified at \u00a71.5-2.\n\n4-3. Expected price = 0.25\u00d7230 + 0.50\u00d7425 + 0.25\u00d7545 = $406.25 \u2192 $406\n [Arithmetic]. 50% CI \u2248 $330\u2013$500; 80% CI \u2248 $230\u2013$545 [Estimate].\n4-4. Margin of safety: (425 \u2212 350.73) / 425 = 17.5% \u2192 ADEQUATE.\n4-5. Note the asymmetry: at T0, Bear is only \u221234%, Bull is +55%, Base +21%.\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n4.5-1/2. Market-implied horizon: at the market's implied discount rate\n~9.4% (or ~2.0% terminal growth at my WACC), Tyler is being priced as a\nlow-growth, high-risk utility. My horizon is 8 explicit years plus a 3.5%\nterminal fade, giving a base value $425. The spread between my horizon and\nthe market's is ~1.0\u20131.4 points of discount rate \u2014 that IS the arbitrage.\nIt can close via price appreciation to $425+ (if rates stabilize) or via my\nbeing wrong about WACC (if the 10Y crosses ~5.2%).\n I do not rely on a 12-month re-rating to justify the position; I rely on\n3\u20135-year cash flows and on non-GAAP EPS growing ~13\u201317% per year [company\nguidance on EPS growth 2025\u21922026; consensus 17.2% [Aggregator]].\n At a constant 26.8x forward non-GAAP multiple, FY2030 EPS of ~$18\u201322\n [Estimate] gives a 3\u20135-year total return of roughly +50\u201390%.\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n5-1. Conviction score (total /15):\n 1. Business quality: 3 (moat 13/21 wide, financial strength excellent)\n 2. Valuation attractiveness: 2 (MoS 17.5% adequate; bottom-decile multiple;\n    but GAAP P/E is 42x \u2014 this is NOT a deep-value mispricing)\n 3. Macro alignment: 2 (defensive demand in a stagflation-heavy world;\n    long-duration rate risk)\n 4. Thesis clarity: 3 (measurable KPIs: recurring growth \u22658%, SaaS growth\n    \u226518%, op margin trajectory to mid-30s, owner FCF margin \u226521%)\n 5. Execution traceability: 2 (consistent guidance, beat EPS, revenue\n    slightly missed; buyback discipline)\n TOTAL: 12/15 \u2192 HIGH\n5-2. Conviction decay: \u22120 since thesis is new at T0. [Framework rule]\n5-3. Sizing: High conviction \u2192 5\u20138% guideline. Adjusted:\n - Crowding (Layer 5.5): \u22121%p \u2192 4\u20137%\n - Macro rate risk: keep at lower half \u2192 5% target cap.\n FINAL: Target 5% of portfolio, built in tranches (see Section 12).\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n - Short interest: 9.94% of float [Aggregator, Aug 14 2026]; days to cover\n   4.37; short interest +2.5pt month-over-month \u2192 bearish positioning\n   building; moderate squeeze potential on any good news\n - Institutional ownership: 107.28% [Aggregator] \u2014 very high; passive/ETF\n   flows dominate \u2192 Tyler is vulnerable to software-sector flow pressure\n   regardless of fundamentals\n - Insider ownership: 0.52% [Aggregator]; recent 6-mo cluster: [Unconfirmed]\n - Options positioning: no data [Unconfirmed]\n - Sentiment: stock \u221239% from 52-wk high; sell-side mean target $426.64 vs\n   price $350.73 [Aggregator] \u2014 analysts are NOT bearish; the tape is.\n   This is a mild contrarian positive (crowd has capitulated at the price\n   level, even if targets lag)\n - Crowding Risk Score: 9/15 (high inst. ownership + sector crowding unwind\n   + elevated short) \u2192 position size \u22121%p implemented in Section 5.\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n - Beta 0.809 [Aggregator] \u2192 lower correlation than pure software names\n - End-market (US public sector) is distinct from commercial software books\n - In a stagflation scenario (45% prob): Tyler is one of the better software\n   names to own; budgets are mandatory\n - In a rate-shock scenario: hurts like any 27x P/E stock\n - Suggested treatment: if the portfolio is heavy in\n   Microsoft/Oracle/Accenture or in commercial SaaS, keep Tyler at the lower\n   end (4%); if the portfolio is defensively positioned, 5% is appropriate.\n [Assumption about the rest of the portfolio]\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n - Scenario outcomes from \u00a74: +55% (25%), +21% (50%), \u221234% (25%)\n - Edge = 0.25\u00d70.55 + 0.50\u00d70.21 \u2212 0.25\u00d70.34 = +0.1575 (15.75%) [Arithmetic]\n - Variance = 0.25\u00d7(0.55)\u00b2 + 0.50\u00d7(0.21)\u00b2 + 0.25\u00d7(0.34)\u00b2 \u2212 (0.1575)\u00b2\n   = 0.0756 + 0.0221 + 0.0289 \u2212 0.0248 = 0.1018 [Arithmetic]\n - Full Kelly \u2248 0.1575 / 0.1018 \u2248 155% \u2192 cap required. With 1/4-Kelly:\n   ~39% \u2192 STILL above any single-stock cap. CONCLUSION: Kelly is NOT the\n   binding constraint; the Layer-5 cap (5%) dominates.\n - Framework check \"L5 vs L6.5 gap >20%p\": I re-analyzed and confirm that\n   the 5% cap is the correct risk control: Kelly's assumptions (known odds,\n   unbounded capital, no liquidation risk) break down on a single stock.\n   PASSED.\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n7-1. Catalysts (next 12 months):\n - Q3'26 earnings (late Oct 2026): re-affirmation of FY26 guided range\n   [$2.535\u20132.575B revenue; EPS $12.95\u201313.20 [Company guidance]]\n - Investor Day 2030 detail follow-through; record SaaS bookings already\n   cited for Q2 [Unconfirmed\u2014research brief]\n - $1.5B buyback execution: at the current price, that is ~4.3M shares\n   (~10.5% of equity) of EPS accretion [Arithmetic]\n - FTR integration / court-ecosystem cross-sell [Company]\n - AI-in-government announcements (transforms cost narrative) [Unconfirmed]\n7-2. Key risks:\n - Rate shock: 10Y above ~5.2% \u2192 WACC 8.5%+ \u2192 fair value ~$330\u2013380\n - Stagflation deepening: state/local budget austerity cuts into new\n   projects; total revenue growth below 6%\n - AI-budget cannibalization narrative persists \u2192 multiple stays in the\n   low-20s forward\n - Competitive bundle from Microsoft/Oracle at lower cost \u2192 mid-market share\n   loss\n - GAAP/non-GAAP wedge: if SBC continues to grow faster than revenue,\n   GAAP EPS quality deteriorates (FY25 SBC $151M = 47% of GAAP net income)\n - Convertible dilution if stock > ~$550 (adds ~2.4\u20132.6M shares) [Estimate]\n - Legal: Kaskela Law investigation and ransomware lawsuit \u2014 [Unconfirmed]\n   from the research brief; I could not verify independently. CARRYING as\n   uncertainty, not as a known liability.\n7-3. Pre-mortem: \"It is 2031 and TYL is at $200.\" Why?\n (a) State/local fiscal crisis in 2027\u201328 \u2192 growth fell to 2\u20133%; or\n (b) a major cyber incident caused a large liability and a 12-month sales\n     freeze; or\n (c) Microsoft/Oracle bundle took the mid-market; or\n (d) management overpaid M&A and diluted the franchise.\n EARLY WARNING INDICATORS (to monitor quarterly):\n - recurring revenue growth < 6% for two consecutive quarters\n - SaaS bookings growth < 5-7%\n - retention below ~93% [Assumption \u2014 actual retention not in pack]\n - GAAP operating margin < 14%\n - owner FCF margin < 19%\n - large insider sell cluster [Unconfirmed \u2014 monitor]\n - new conversion rate of cloud bookings to revenue < 70% [Estimate]\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n - Common bear trigger 1 (Rates up): affects all long-duration holdings.\n   Tyler's beta of 0.809 means it falls LESS than the software complex, but\n   it will still fall. Mitigation: position cap 5%.\n - Common bear trigger 2 (Stagflation): Tyler is relatively defensive.\n - Common bear trigger 3 (AI-capex crash): Tyler is LESS exposed than\n   commercial software (government demand, not AI).\n - Tail correlation: in a 2008-style liquidity crisis, all correlated assets\n   sell off; estimated CVaR for this position: \u221245% to \u221255%\n   [Estimate \u2014 consistent with software multiple compression to 15\u201318x and\n   a modest EPS cut].\n - Contribution to portfolio tail risk: LOW given 5% weight.\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nTHESIS KPIs (quarterly; breach triggers re-evaluation):\n 1. Total revenue growth \u2265 8%; recurring revenue growth \u2265 8%;\n    SaaS revenue growth \u2265 18% [Q2 2026 actual: SaaS +21.7% [Company IR]]\n 2. Non-GAAP FCF margin within guided 26\u201328% FY26 [Company guidance];\n    OWNER FCF margin \u2265 21%\n 3. GAAP operating margin \u2265 15%\n 4. Transaction revenue does NOT go negative YoY (Q2'26: +3.5%)\n 5. Buyback run-rate \u2265 $100M/quarter at prices below $425\n 6. 10Y Treasury: if > 5.2% for a month, re-run WACC scenario; if < 4.0%,\n    consider adding.\n 7. Watch: SEC filings on Kaskela investigation and ransomware lawsuit;\n    conversion of new cloud bookings to revenue.\n\nRE-EVALUATION TRIGGERS:\n - Any breach of two KPIs in the same quarter \u2192 downgrade conviction to\n   Medium and halve the position.\n - Q3'26 revenue guidance < $640M (i.e., flat QoQ) \u2192 thesis broken.\n - Non-GAAP FCF margin < 24% in FY26 \u2192 thesis broken.\n - 10Y > 5.5% \u2192 liquidation of 1/3 of the position (valuation floor\n   $330\u2013350).\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n9-1. Short thesis (using the same data):\n 1. The 41.7x trailing GAAP P/E is not defensible for an 8% grower; the\n    non-GAAP 26.8x forward P/E excludes SBC of $151M/yr (47% of FY25 GAAP\n    net income). GAAP EPS was $7.21; an investor is paying 48.6x GAAP\n    earnings for a company growing net income +10% YoY.\n 2. The $1.4375B 0.50% convertible (issued May 2026 [Company\u2014BusinessWire])\n    is 10% of market cap. The company raised money while buying shares \u2014\n    effectively re-leveraging the balance sheet at a market top; the entire\n    maneuver is a bet that the stock was cheap at $350\u2013450.\n 3. Growth is decelerating: Q4'25 +6.3%, Q1'26 +8.6%, Q2'26 +8.2%. The\n    \"double-digit\" story is over; FY26 guide implies a H2 acceleration that\n    relies on acquisitions (FTR) and undisclosed bookings.\n 4. Transaction revenue +3.5% \u2014 payments volume is slowing; AI-powered\n    government self-service (chatbots) may structurally reduce per-\n    transaction revenue.\n 5. State/local fiscal cliff after COVID-era federal aid exhaustion \u2192\n    IT budgets will compress exactly when Tyler needs to fund its\n    transition to mid-market.\n 6. The Kaskela investigation and a ransomware/data-breach lawsuit create\n    overhangs [Unconfirmed].\n 7. Software sector de-rating on AI-cannibalization is NOT finished: the\n    sector's multiple resets tend to undershoot, and Tyler is still 26.8x\n    forward.\n9-2. Logic combat:\n - Long argument strength: 7/10\n - Short argument strength: 6/10\n Gap = 1 (< 2) \u2192 framework requires conviction downgrade of \u22120.5 steps.\n APPLIED: final conviction 11.5/15.\n The shorts' strongest point: the GAAP wedge and the deceleration. My\n honest reply: both are real, and both are partially in the price (the\n stock fell 39%; it now trades at 26.8x forward non-GAAP vs a 3-year\n average closer to 35x [Estimate]).\n9-3. Adversarial data: no Hindenburg-style report found for TYL.\n The Kaskela investigation and ransomware lawsuit [Unconfirmed] are the\n only named adversarial items; I could not verify them and did not\n materially adjust the valuation for them. If either produces a material\n settlement (>$200M), reduce fair value by ~$5/share [Arithmetic].\n9-4. Killed arguments:\n - \"AI kills Tyler\": only if AI replaces the need for workflow-specific\n   government software AND Tyler fails to embed AI. Monitor: AI-product\n   announcements and renewal rates. Not validated today.\n - \"The buyback is bad capital allocation\": it is value-accretive only if\n   the shares are below IV. At $350 vs IV $425, it is rational. Killed for\n   now, re-examine below $300 (then it is more accretive, not less).\n - \"Microsoft/Oracle will crush them\": there is no evidence of share loss\n   in the filed numbers. Killed until evidence appears.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nThe 37 RS2 v2.0 audit points were applied. The most material checks are\nshown:\n\nA. METHODOLOGY\n \u2713 Archetype B selected with reasons; Engine 1 + cross-checks; not DCF-only\n \u2713 No peer multiple-only; no TAM-only; macro incorporated\nB. VALUATION\n \u2713 FCF definition: owner FCF (OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC) used; SBC treated as real\n \u2713 Working capital anomaly identified and corrected (convertible\n   reclassification) \u2014 see Section 0\n \u2713 WACC fully derived; sensitivity table shown\n \u2713 Reverse DCF performed; market-implied WACC quantified (~9.4%)\n \u2713 Terminal value shown; not a plug\n \u2713 Explicit 8-year forecast shown, year-by-year, with tagged sources\nC. BIAS\n \u2713 Anchoring check: I verified sell-side mean $426 vs my base $425 BEFORE\n   finalizing \u2014 this converges, but I re-audited my growth path and kept it\n   below company guidance to avoid confirmation bias\n \u2713 Overconfidence check: 80% CI is wide ($230\u2013545) and reflects my\n   admitted uncertainty on WACC and on legal items\n \u2713 Red-team gap applied: conviction reduced \u22120.5 steps\nD. CONSISTENCY\n \u2713 Scenario probabilities sum to 100%; base is the highest probability\n \u2713 L5 (5%) vs L6.5 (Kelly >20%) gap re-analyzed; 5% cap justified\n \u2713 Time-point discipline: T0 = 2026-08-24; financials as of TTM Q2'26;\n   did not mix FY25 balance sheet with T0 values without adjustment\nE. NARRATIVE\n \u2713 \"Tyler 2030\" targets used as a cross-check only, not as an input\n \u2713 No phrases like \"large long-term potential\" used\nF. RED TEAM\n \u2713 Short thesis written using same data; logic gap quantified; Killed\n   Arguments section present\nG. DATA INTEGRITY\n \u2713 Series continuity warning heeded: the FY2025 WC/current-liability step\n   was investigated and explained\n \u2713 Vendor capex vs filed capex: flagged; did not silently pick one\n \u2713 Debt components not