u00b1 0.5pp) | $300 | $350 | \u00b18% |\n| 2034 FCF margin (23% \u00b1 1pp) | $300 | $350 | \u00b18% |\n| 2030 revenue (3.59 \u00b1 0.3B) | $300 | $350 | \u00b18% |\nTop 3 thesis KPIs: (1) owner-FCF margin, (2) revenue CAGR 2026\u201330, (3) WACC /\n10-year rate.\n\nMULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK (Engine discipline \u2014 no DCF-only conclusion):\n- FY2026 non-GAAP EPS: $12.95\u201313.20 [Actual guidance] \u2192 fwd P/E = 26.6\u201327.1x\n  [Arithmetic]\n- Fair P/E for a 10% grower with 26% FCF margins at 4.71% 10Y: 22\u201325x\n  [Assumption \u2014 sourced from a standard P/E/growth/rate framework, not\n  retrieved] \u2192 fair value $285\u2013330\n- GAAP TTM EPS $7.42 [Arithmetic] \u2192 trailing GAAP P/E 47.3x; fair 35\u201340x \u2192\n  $260\u2013297\n- Reverse DCF at $350.73: EV = ($350.73 \u00d7 0.042) + 0.90 = $15.63B. Required\n  owner-FCF 2034 = 15.63 / (PV exit factor 0.4632 \u00d7 (1/(r-g)) + PV flow factor)\n  \u2248 $1.28B organic \u2014 i.e. the market requires the bull case: 9.5%+ growth through\n  2034 AND a 25% owner-FCF margin. Feasible, but a high bar.\nMultiples verdict: consistent with DCF \u2014 fair value $285\u2013330, midpoint ~$308;\nmy DCF base $324 is at the top of that range because the model gives credit for\nthe cloud transition the market has already partly de-rated.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 3.5-1 Competitive Response: (-1) If Tyler succeeds with AI/transaction\nrevenue, Oracle and Microsoft Gov Cloud will respond with subsidized bundles.\nImpact is moderate because procurement cycles are slow and Tyler\u2019s data incumbency\nis protected by 30 years of migrations; but price pressure in mid-sized counties\n(where Tyler is 30\u201360% more expensive per research brief [Estimate]) could\nintensify.\n\nStep 3.5-2 Reflexivity: MILD POSITIVE today. The 39% drawdown [Arithmetic on\n52-wk range] reduces SBC attractiveness and the acquisition currency; but the\ncompany\u2019s >5.5% buyback at lower prices is a positive reflexive signal of\ninsider belief. A falling stock does NOT endanger Tyler\u2019s operations (no debt\ncovenant risk, mission-critical government contracts are sticky) \u2014 negative\nreflexivity is lower than at typical high-beta software.\n\nStep 3.5-3 Customer Evolution: The early adopter segment is done. The mainstream\nis now converting: ERP version currency 85\u201390% [Actual] and court legacy down to\n7% [Actual]. As penetration grows, LTV/CAC will worsen slightly (new logos are\nsmaller, mid-sized deals \u2014 \u201chigh volume of mid-sized deals rather than large\nmega contracts\u201d [Actual]) and churn could rise at the low end. This is already\nembedded in my assumption that growth fades to 4% by 2034.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRIOR (Layer 1.5): Large-cap software achieves ~10% growth + margin expansion\nfor 5 years with ~40\u201360% probability [Estimate]. Tyler\u2019s specific transition\nstatus (85\u201390% version current) updates the prior upward.\n\nPOSTERIOR UPDATE (evidence since 2025: raised FY2026 guidance [Actual], record\nSaaS bookings [Actual], 40+ AP wins [Actual], AI revenue ramp pushed to 2H2027\n[Actual \u2014 Q2 call], FTR closed [Actual]):\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Revenue 2030 | Owner-FCF margin 2034 | WACC | IV/share |\n|----------|------|--------------|------------------------|------|----------|\n| Disaster: breach/regulatory/AI disruption; budgets freeze | 5% | $2.8B | 16% | 8.5% | $120 |\n| Bear: growth 5%, margins flat, 10Y >5% | 20% | $3.25B | 20% | 8.5% | $205 |\n| Base: 8.8% CAGR 26\u201330, margin 23% | 55% | $3.59B | 23% | 8.0% | $324 |\n| Bull: 11% CAGR, AI/transactions accelerate, SBC fades | 20% | $4.30B | 25% | 7.75% | $525 |\nTotal: 100%. Base is the single highest probability. Constraints respected: no\nsingle-point, no rounding off of 5% tail risk.\n\nEXPECTED PRICE = 0.05\u00d7120 + 0.20\u00d7205 + 0.55\u00d7324 + 0.20\u00d7525 = $331 [Arithmetic]\nMARGIN OF SAFETY (base) = (324 - 350.73)/324 = -8.3% \u2192 NONE.\nJudgment: Limited to None. The price is 8% above base IV and the expected value\nis 6% below price. A value investor waits.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMarket-implied horizon: at $350.73, the market pays 27x forward non-GAAP EPS on\na 9.5% grower \u2014 that implies ~10 years of growth continuation at the guided\ntrajectory and a 25% exit FCF margin [Arithmetic/Estimate]. The market is\nvaluing the FULL Tyler 2030 plan without haircut. My horizon (3\u20135 years) sees\nrevenue $2.8\u20133.6B and FCF $0.59\u20130.81B \u2014 the equity value under MY 5-year view is\nEV = PV(5 yrs FCF) + PV(terminal at conservative margins) \u2248 0.485+0.506+0.522+\n0.537+0.549 + (0.807\u00d71.03/(0.08-0.03) \u00d7 0.6806) = 2.60 + 11.32 = $13.9B \u2192 ~$310\u2013330\nper share [Estimate]. Spread = My horizon value minus market value \u2248 -$20 to -$40\nper share. NO horizon arbitrage exists at T0; the market\u2019s error is not in\nhorizon but in margin (it is paying for the bull case).\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 5-1 Conviction Score (0-15):\n- Business quality: 3/3 (wide moat, 21.7% SaaS growth, 26\u201328% FCF margin guidance)\n- Valuation attractiveness: 1/3 (MoS -8.3%; fwd P/E 27x non-GAAP, 47x GAAP)\n- Macro alignment: 1/3 (10Y at 4.71% and Fed hawkish is a headwind to\n  long-duration software)\n- Thesis clarity: 3/3 (quantified KPIs: SaaS growth, cloud conversion, FCF\n  margin, non-GAAP EPS, AI ARR)\n- Execution traceability: 3/3 (raised FY2026 guide [Actual]; record bookings and\n  FCF [Actual]; >5.5% buyback)\nTOTAL = 11/15 \u2192 HIGH. Step 5-2 decay (-1 every 6 months) \u2192 10. Macro headwind\n(-0.5) \u2192 9.5. Layer 9 logic-gap (1.0) \u2192 -1 \u2192 8.5 \u2192 EFFECTIVE CONVICTION: MEDIUM\n(7\u20139).\n\nStep 5-3 Sizing:\n- Medium conviction max weight: 6% portfolio\n- Macro headwind cap: -1pp \u2192 5%\n- Negative MoS: NO NEW POSITION; existing holders cap at 4% (audited decision,\n  see Section 12).\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 5.5 \u2014 BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n- Short interest: 9.94% of float, 3.499M shares, 4.37 days to cover, MoM +2.5\n  [Aggregator \u2013 2026-08-14]. The research brief cites 8.46% on 2026-07-15\n  [Estimate \u2013 MarketBeat]; direction (rising) agrees. Short interest at 10% in a\n  stock down 39% is a CROWDED-SHORT signal \u2014 a meaningful squeeze fuel, but not\n  a valuation input.\n- Institutional ownership: 107.28% [Aggregator \u2013 13F double counting is likely].\n  Passive/ETF ownership is heavy \u2192 vulnerability to software-sector outflows is\n  HIGH. This partially explains the 46% drawdown [Estimate \u2013 TIKR headline].\n- Insider: 0.52% ownership [Aggregator]; no net cluster buying [Unconfirmed].\n- Options: not retrieved [Unconfirmed].\n- Sentiment: consensus mean target $426.64 / median $427.50 vs price $350.73 \u2014\n  sell-side is 21% above market, suggesting the \u201csmart money\u201d has already\n  de-rated faster than analysts. 52-week range $270.71\u2013$578.19 [Actual]; the\n  low is 23% below today.\n- Crowding Risk Score: 9/15 (high passive ownership, 10% short interest, recent\n  momentum unwind, heavy SBC supply). Position size reduction: -1pp if held.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 6 \u2014 PORTFOLIO FIT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTYL is a low-beta (0.809 [Aggregator \u2013 vendor]) quality compounder with\ncountercyclical revenue (government budgets are stickier than corporate IT). It\nfits a portfolio as a DEFENSIVE GROWTH sleeve, not a high-conviction growth\nholding. Its correlation to long-duration software is high (r ~0.7+ [Estimate]);\ndo NOT add it alongside other high-multiple SaaS names expecting diversification.\nIn a stagflation/recession mix (55% probability in my regime distribution),\nmission-critical government software is one of the better places to hide \u2014 but\nthe entry price destroys that protection.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 6.5 \u2014 KELLY SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKelly formula: f = (p \u00d7 b - q) / b, with b = (IV/Price - 1):\n- Base case: b = (324/350.73 - 1) = -0.076 \u2192 NEGATIVE edge \u2192 f = 0%.\n- Expected-value case: E[V]/Price = 331/350.73 = 0.944 \u2192 b = -0.056 \u2192 f = 0%.\n- At $295 (10% MoS): b = 0.098; p = 0.55, q = 0.45 \u2192 f = (0.55\u00d71.098 - 0.45)/\n  1.098 = (0.604 - 0.45)/1.098 = 0.140 \u2192 half-Kelly 7% \u2192 capped at 4% by macro\n  headwind and crowding \u2192 TARGET WEIGHT 4% at $295.\nLayer 5 (5%) vs Kelly (0%) gap = 5pp < 20pp \u2192 no re-analysis required, but\nconvergence at $295 is the action threshold.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 7 \u2014 RISKS & CATALYSTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCATALYSTS (next 12 months):\n1. FY2026 EPS delivery vs raised guide $12.95\u201313.20 [Actual \u2014 means 2H26 needs\n   ~$6.5 EPS vs $5.65 H1 [Estimate]; possible but back-loaded)\n2. AI revenue ramp beginning 2H2027 [Actual \u2013 Q2 call] \u2014 early ARR prints\n   (multi-million per state [Actual])\n3. Cloud conversion progress: 85% of 2023 maintenance base by 2030 [Actual]\n4. Continued buybacks (>5.5% YTD [Actual])\n5. Convertible/capped-call details: cap price NOT retrieved [Unconfirmed];\n   a cap above $520 would be accretive if the stock recovers.\n\nRISKS:\n1. 10Y >5% or Fed hike \u2192 multiple compression (27x \u2192 22x = -19% downside\n   [Arithmetic])\n2. Data-breach litigation: Kaskela investigation announced [Unconfirmed \u2013 research\n   brief]; a second major breach would be a governance shock to a government-\n   trust franchise.\n3. Texas payments contract decline [Actual guide notes 1.8pp drag]\n4. SBC dilution: 2.0% 3-yr CAGR [Arithmetic]; if buybacks stop, per-share FCF\n   growth halves.\n5. Competitive: Oracle/MSFT Gov Cloud price pressure [Estimate \u2013 brief]\n6. Convertible maturity/refinancing at 2031 [Actual]; at 0.5% coupon, low\n   financial risk, but total debt $2.0B+ is new for this company.\n\nPRE-MORTEM (5 years from now, this failed \u2014 why?):\n1. The AI-native competitor (MSFT Copilot for Government) let agencies bypass\n   Tyler\u2019s workflows; switching costs collapsed as AI made data portable.\n   Early warning: SaaS growth <10% for 2 consecutive quarters.\n2. A cyber breach on Tyler\u2019s SaaS cloud caused a multi-state exodus.\n   Early warning: new large-deal win rate below 30% and rising security\n   spend with no new logo growth.\n3. The leveraged buyback bet failed: the stock fell to $250 (as macro shocked\n   multiples) while the converts stayed out-of-the-money, amplifying EPS\n   decline. Early warning: 10Y >5.5% and non-GAAP EPS guide cut.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n- Common bear driver: a discount-rate shock (10Y rising) hits TYL AND every\n  long-duration holding simultaneously. The 2026 drawdown is already a live\n  example (stock -39% while the 10Y +44bp y/y [Arithmetic]).\n- If the portfolio holds MSFT/ADBE/CRM/PLTR, the tail correlation in a 2022-style\n  repricing is ~0.8 [Estimate]. TYL provides no hedge in that scenario.\n- CVaR at 5% (from scenario distribution): ~$120/share = -66% [Estimate].\n- Mitigation: cap software beta-adjusted exposure at 15\u201320% gross; avoid TYL as\n  a \"diversifier.\"\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 8 \u2014 MONITORING RULES\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHEsis KPI THRESHOLDS (review quarterly after 10-Q):\n| KPI | Current | Watch level | Break level |\n|-----|---------|-------------|-------------|\n| SaaS revenue growth | +21.7% [Actual] | >15% | <10% |\n| Total revenue growth ex-Texas | ~11.3% guided [Actual] | >9% | <6% |\n| Non-GAAP EPS vs guide | $12.95\u201313.20 [Actual] | within range | below range |\n| Owner-FCF margin (post-SBC) | 20.9% [Arithmetic] | >20% | <18% |\n| Mgmt-defined FCF margin | 26\u201328% guide [Actual] | 24\u201328% | <23% |\n| Cloud conversion of 2023 base | N/A [Unconfirmed %] | on track | delayed >2 qtrs |\n| AI ARR | \"multiple millions per state\" [Actual] | >$10M annual by 2027 | no disclosed ARR |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | ~1.9x [Estimate] | <2.5x | >3.0x |\n| Buyback pace | >5.5% YTD [Actual] | \u22651% per qtr | stopped entirely |\nRE-EVALUATION TRIGGERS: MoS \u2265 +10% (price \u2264 $295) \u2192 re-size; 10Y \u2265 5.25%;\nSaaS growth <10%; owner-FCF margin <18%; any second data breach.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 9 \u2014 RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 9-1 SHORT THESIS (from the same data):\n1. GAAP quality is poor: TTM NI $0.325B vs OCF $0.731B [Arithmetic], SBC $0.151B\n   (6.5% of revenue) [Actual]. Non-GAAP EPS $12.95\u201313.20 [Actual] vs GAAP ~$8.70\n   [Estimate] \u2014 a ~50% gap. The \u201chigh-quality\u201d label fails on GAAP.\n2. At 27x forward non-GAAP EPS and 47x trailing GAAP EPS [Arithmetic], the\n   market pays for the 2030 plan; any slippage (AI, budgets, competition)\n   compresses the stock to $250 (\u201329%).\n3. The company issued $1.25B\u20131.4375B of converts [Actual/Unconfirmed] at 0.5%\n   to repurchase stock near $350 [Estimate]. If the stock stays flat, this\n   leverage was a value transfer to the converts (dilution if it rises, cash\n   burn if it falls). A leveraged buyback at a rich price is value-destructive.\n4. AI-driven interfaces reduce workflow lock-in; Microsoft/Oracle can undercut\n   Tyler\u2019s 30\u201360% price premium [Estimate] in mid-sized counties.\n5. Cybersecurity: confirmed prior breach litigation is still active\n   [Unconfirmed \u2013 research brief]; Kaskela investigation adds governance\n   overhang [Unconfirmed].\n6. Growth is slowing organically: total revenue decel 10.2%\u21928.2% [Arithmetic;\n   Q2 2026]; ex-Texas organic growth only ~2pp below total (i.e. ~8%\n   organic) [Actual]. The 21.7% SaaS growth is flattered by mix shift, not\n   end-market acceleration.\n7. Institutional ownership 107% + 9.9% short interest = a crowded unwind risk\n   with no insider support (0.52% insider ownership).\n\nStep 9-2 LOGIC COMBAT:\n- LONG: 6.5/10 \u2014 moat is real, FCF machine is real, management execution is\n  above-average, and government budgets are structurally supportive.\n- SHORT: 5.5/10 \u2014 valuation and GAAP quality arguments are strong, but the short\n  case lacks a credible share-loss catalyst today; Oracle has not won\n  meaningful local-government ERP share in the past decade of trying.\n- GAP: 1.0 \u2192 Conviction downgrade of 1 point (already applied in Section 5).\n\nStep 9-3 ADVERSARIAL DATA