nt\nExpected Macro Impact = 0.30(Goldilocks:+0.5) + 0.40(Reflation:\u22120.5) + 0.20(Stagflation:+0)\n + 0.10(Recession:+0.5) \u2248 \u22120.1 \u2192 NEUTRAL, mild headwind.\nNo conviction change from macro. Split-entry strategy strengthened because of\nquantitative-tightening/late-cycle tape.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base-rate inquiry\n\u2022 5-year survival of a stable incumbent software firm with 86% recurring revenue: ~95%\n  [Assumption based on public-company survival statistics].\n\u2022 Probability that a 10% grower with 27% EBITDA margin and narrow moat compounds EPS\n  at 10%+ for 5 years: ~70% [Assumption].\n\u2022 Probability that a decelerating growth/momentum stock (down 39% from high) continues\n  to de-rate: ~40\u201350% \u2014 consistent with the cyclical-peak base rate of a 40% drawdown\n  within 3 years (~30\u201340%) [Reference base rate from framework].\n\u2022 Large M&A (>$10B) earning ROIC>WACC: N/A (FTR is $0.213B \u2014 small, lower risk).\n\u2022 Platform-transition success (on-prem\u2192SaaS): ~20\u201330% base rate for the industry; Tyler\n  is ~5 years in with 86.7% recurring revenue \u2014 already demonstrably successful\n  [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 PR], so we condition upward.\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate\nOur base case (EPS CAGR ~11\u201312%) is slightly above the broad software base rate (~8\u201310%)\nbut justified by three company-specific facts:\n1. ARR is $2.24B and growing 8.2% \u2014 a visible contractual base, not a narrative [Actual].\n2. SaaS bookings at record levels with 22 consecutive quarters of \u226520% SaaS growth\n   [Actual \u2014 Q2'26 PR].\n3. Guided FCF margin 26\u201328% with >100% conversion of adjusted net income \u2014 a\n   demonstrated cash machine [Actual \u2014 FY26 guidance].\nDeviation is modest; no red flag.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check\nSuccess probability used (78%) is ~1.1\u00d7 base rate \u2014 below the 1.5\u00d7 threshold. No\nadditional evidence required.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences)\nTyler Technologies designs, implements, and supports enterprise software for U.S. local\nand state government \u2014 financials/ERP, courts, public safety, permits, records, and\ndigital payments. It generates ~86.7% recurring revenue from a 16,000-client installed\nbase with 12\u201324 month implementation cycles that create very high switching costs. Its\ngrowth is driven by the multi-decade migration of on-premise licenses to SaaS, an\nexpanding transactions/payments business, and now AI tools (e.g. For The Record, a\nlegal-grade AI transcription platform acquired April 2026 for $212.7M [Actual]).\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment + dynamics (0\u20133 each)\n\u2022 Intangible assets (brand/domain): 2 \u2014 trusted public-sector brand, niche vertical IP\n\u2022 Switching costs: 3 \u2014 mission-critical, long implementations, data lock-in\n\u2022 Network effects: 1 \u2014 cross-agency data/court networks, but not classic network effects\n\u2022 Cost advantage: 1 \u2014 not a low-cost producer (30\u201360% premium vs niche competitors)\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2 \u2014 R&D spread over large base; benefits from breadth\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers: 2 \u2014 government procurement, security/compliance certifications\n\u2022 Data moat: 2 \u2014 public-sector datasets + AI training edge\nMoat score: 13/21 \u2192 WIDE moat (low end) [Estimate \u2014 qualitative scoring].\nMoat direction: WIDENING (SaaS mix rising, record bookings, AI-driven feature depth,\n22 consecutive quarters of \u226520% SaaS growth) \u2014 but watch price-sensitive mid-market\nthreats from Civic Systems/Caselle and platform giants (Oracle, Microsoft).\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\nSTALWART. Revenue CAGR (TTM) ~8\u201310%, GAAP ROE ~9.7%, steady margins, negligible\ndividend but aggressive buybacks. Checkpoints: (a) strong balance sheet \u2014 yes\n(cash+investments >$1B post-convert [Actual Q2'26 PR]); (b) defensible niche \u2014 yes;\n(c) not over-priced relative to growth \u2014 borderline: 26.8x forward non-GAAP EPS vs\n~11% EPS growth \u2192 PEG ~2.4, rich for a Stalwart.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n\u2022 Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR (FY22\u219225): +8.0% [Arithmetic on filed] | TTM growth: +8.2%\n  [Actual]\n\u2022 Operating margin (FY25 GAAP): 15.4%; 12-yr median: 15.4% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 EPS growth: FY24 +58% (GAAP), FY25 +20% (GAAP) [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 ROE (GAAP, vendor): 9.7% [Estimate] | ROIC (GAAP): ~8.5% [Estimate \u2014 see Section 2.5]\n\u2022 FCF margin (TTM, company definition): 29.4% (0.714/2.430) [Actual]\n\u2022 FCF conversion vs OCF: 0.714/0.731 = 97.7% [Actual]\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: ~1.3x (est. $0.9B net debt \u00f7 $0.70B adj. EBITDA) [Estimate]\n\u2022 Interest coverage: net interest INCOME of $19\u201321M guided FY26 [Actual] \u2014 strong\nJudgment: EXCELLENT cash generation; ADEQUATE GAAP profitability.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 $505M repurchased in Q2'26 (~1.62M sh at ~$311, below\n   current price and far below 52-wk high); $1.745B remaining [Actual]. But some\n   buybacks debt-funded via convert \u2014 less clean.\n2. M&A track record: 1/3 \u2014 FTR ($212.7M) is the 3rd-largest acquisition ever; no long\n   track record of the 2026 strategy; historical M&A mixed [Estimate].\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 tiny capex ($18\u201320M guided incl. $6M capitalized software);\n   incremental ROIC on capital high because the model is asset-light.\n4. Mix rationality: 2/3 \u2014 simultaneous $1.4B convert + record buybacks + M&A is\n   aggressive/opportunistic; coherent only if stock is truly undervalued.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 beat Q2'26 non-GAAP EPS ($3.08 vs $2.95 consensus); raised\n   FY26 non-GAAP EPS guidance ($12.95\u201313.20 vs 12.30 consensus).\nTotal: 10/15. Insider ownership 0.52% [Actual] \u2014 no bonus. No insiders net-buying signal\n[Unconfirmed]. Score: 10/15.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization\nFY26E R&D: $245\u2013250M guided [Actual]. Treat 4-year straight-line amortization:\n\u2022 Adjusted operating income (FY25) = GAAP OI 358 + R&D (~230) \u2212 R&D amort (~58)\n  \u2248 $530M [Estimate]\n\u2022 Unamortized R&D asset (FY25, ~4 yrs \u00d7 ~230 \u00d7 avg 2 yrs) \u2248 $460M [Estimate]\n\u2022 Adjusted invested capital = 3.703+0.600\u22121.015+0.460 \u2248 $3.75B [Estimate]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC as cash expense\n\u2022 FY25 SBC: $151M [Actual]. Adjusted FCF (FY25) = 654 \u2212 16 \u2212 151 = $487M [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 TTM adjusted FCF = 731 \u2212 16 \u2212 ~155 \u2248 $560M [Estimate]\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases: FY25 operating leases $43M [Actual]; add to debt\n\u2192 Adjusted net debt incl. leases \u2248 $0.94B [Estimate].\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data asset: not modeled \u2014 public-sector brand is defensible but no\nreliable market valuation; skip to avoid false precision [Unconfirmed].\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nAdjusted NOPAT ~= 530 \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.23) \u2248 $408M; Adjusted IC \u2248 $3.75B\n\u2192 Adjusted ROIC \u2248 10.9% vs GAAP ROIC \u2248 8.5%. Gap of 2.4pts explains R&D expensing.\nStill below a quality-software threshold (>20%); the gap to WACC (~8.5%) is modest.\nConclusion: the business earns roughly its cost of capital on a GAAP basis and\n~2.4pts above it on an intangibles-adjusted basis.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett)\nFY25 OE \u2248 NI 316 + D&A 138 + \u0394WC (+287) \u2212 maintenance capex (~30) \u2248 $711M [Estimate];\nbut subtracting SBC (real cost) \u2192 owner earnings \u2248 $560M. Use the conservative figure.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 0 recap: Archetype A/B \u2014 Stable Incumbent with Quality Compounder traits; not\nEngines 2/3/4/5. Not cyclical (Engine 2), not hardware hybrid (3), not option-led (4 \u2014\nthe AI/transactions optionality is material but secondary), not binary (5). Engine 1\nselected.\n\nA. DISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION\n\u2022 Risk-free: 4.71% (10Y UST, 2026-08-21) [Actual]\n\u2022 ERP: 4.75% [Assumption, Damodaran-style large-cap mid-cycle]\n\u2022 Beta: 0.85 [Assumption \u2014 vendor reports 0.809; we use 0.85 to reflect the recent\n  -39% drawdown and software-sector de-rating]\n\u2022 Cost of equity: 4.71 + 0.85 \u00d7 4.75 = 8.75% [Assumption]\n\u2022 Pre-tax cost of debt: 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 convert coupon is 0.50%, but cash-return\n  yield reflecting credit is higher; investment-grade ~5%]\n\u2022 Tax rate: 23% [Assumption \u2014 company's own non-GAAP tax guidance 23.0% [Actual \u2014 IR deck]]\n\u2022 WACC: E/V 94%, D/V 6% \u2192 8.75\u00d70.94 + 3.85\u00d70.06 = 8.45% \u2192 used: 8.5% [Assumption]\n\u2022 Terminal growth: 3.0% (below nominal GDP) [Assumption]\n\u2022 For FCFE (owner cash flows), discount rate = cost of equity = 8.75%.\n\nB. EXPLICIT FORECAST (FY2026E\u2013FY2030E) \u2014 all $B except per-share\nOverriding logic: (1) FY26 revenue = guidance midpoint $2.555B [Actual]; (2) FY27\u201330\ngrowth converges from ~8.5% to ~6.5%, anchored on ARR growth of 8.2% [Actual] \u2014 if ARR\nis the predictor of ~12-month-forward revenue, total growth cannot sustainably exceed\nARR growth; (3) margins expand +30bps annual toward raised 2030 targets (\"substantial\nmargin expansion\" per Morningstar [Estimate]) but we keep this conservative; (4) SBC\ngrows ~10%/yr; (5) share count shrinks ~2.5%/yr due to the $1.745B buyback\nauthorization [Actual].\n\n| FY      | Revenue | Growth | Adj. EBITDA margin | Adj. EBITDA | D&A  | SBC  | Capex | Co. FCF | Owner FCFE | Shares(M) | FCFE/sh |\n|---------|---------|--------|--------------------|-------------|------|------|-------|---------|------------|-----------|---------|\n| 2026E   | 2.555   | +9.6%  | 28.0%              | 0.715       | 0.145| 0.165| 0.019 | 0.690   | 0.525      | 40.9      | 12.83   |\n| 2027E   | 2.772   | +8.5%  | 28.3%              | 0.785       | 0.150| 0.180| 0.021 | 0.765   | 0.585      | 39.9      | 14.65   |\n| 2028E   | 2.980   | +7.5%  | 28.6%              | 0.852       | 0.155| 0.198| 0.022 | 0.835   | 0.637      | 38.9      | 16.38   |\n| 2029E   | 3.189   | +7.0%  | 28.9%              | 0.922       | 0.160| 0.218| 0.024 | 0.905   | 0.687      | 38.0      | 18.08   |\n| 2030E   | 3.396   | +6.5%  | 29.2%              | 0.992       | 0.165| 0.240| 0.026 | 0.975   | 0.735      | 37.1      | 19.81   |\nSourcing: FY26 revenue = guidance [Actual]; growth 27\u201330 = [Assumption] converging from\nARR [Actual]; EBITDA margin from Q2'26 actual 27.6% run-rate [Actual] + margin expansion\n[Assumption]; capex = guidance $18\u201320M [Actual]; D&A [Estimate]; SBC = pack FY25 $151M\n[Actual] + growth [Assumption]. Owner FCFE = Company FCF (OCF\u2212capex, guide 26\u201328% margin\n[Actual]) \u2212 SBC.\n\nC. TERMINAL VALUE\nTV(2030) = FCFE/sh(2030) \u00d7 (1+g)/(CoE \u2212 g) = 19.81 \u00d7 1.04 / (0.0875\u22120.04) =\n$433.7/sh. Terminal value = 71% of IV. Terminal FCFE yield = 4.75% \u2014 reasonable for a\nmature compounder with buybacks.\n\nD. INTRINSIC VALUE (EQUITY, FCFE, PER-SHARE)\nPV(FCFE/sh): 11.80 + 12.39 + 12.73 + 12.92 + 13.02 = $62.86\nPV(TV): 433.7 / (1.0875^5) = $285.1\nIV = $347.9 \u2248 $348/sh [Estimate]\n\nE. CROSS-CHECKS\n\u2022 Reverse DCF: Market EV $15.3B at 8.75%/3% implies perpetuity FCF growth of 4.2% on\n  company FCF (incl. SBC add-back) or 5.3% on owner FCF \u2014 feasible, not stretched.\n\u2022 Multiples (FY26E): P/E non-GAAP 26.8x; EV/EBITDA 21.6x; EV/FCF 22.1x; EV/owner-FCF\n  29.1x; P/S 5.9x \u2014 fair for a 10% grower with 28% EBITDA margin [Estimate].\n\u2022 Reverse (Discounted) P/E: current price implies FY30 non-GAAP EPS of ~$20.6 at 25x \u2014\n  i.e., ~12% EPS CAGR for 4 years. Achievable (8% rev + 1.5% margin + 2.5% buyback).\n\u2022 10-yr median multiples not usable (basis changes; pack continuity warning).\n\nF. TORNADO SENSITIVITY (top 3 variables)\n1. FY30 EBITDA margin: 27.5%\u219229.5% \u21d2 IV $330\u2192$360. (KPI: adj. EBITDA margin)\n2. Revenue CAGR FY27\u201330: 5%\u21929% \u21d2 IV $290\u2192$420. (KPI: ARR growth)\n3. WACC: 8.25%\u21929.25% \u21d2 IV $365\u2192$330. (KPI: 10Y yield / beta)\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response\nIf Tyler successfully dominates (SaaS + AI + transactions), competitors (OpenGov,\nOracle, Microsoft) will respond with lower-priced AI-native modules. Impact: \u22121.5\n(mild negative). If Tyler fails, rivals take mid-market share. The public-sector\nmarket tolerates 2\u20133 vendors \u2014 a price war is less likely than quiet share drift.\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity\nPositive loop: stock price rebound \u2192 SBC attracts talent, M&A currency for FTR-type\ndeals. Negative loop (current): price down 39% \u2192 buyback value trap narrative,\npressure to use debt for buybacks, potential talent retention risk. Net: \u22120.5.\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior evolution\nEarly adopters (counties) already migrated; mainstream now migrating; late adopters\n(small towns) price-sensitive. LTV/CAC may compress at the margin. Retention has been\nstrong; watch for any churn uptick >5% of ARR.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario probabilities (sum = 100%)\n\u2022 BEAR (20%): Public-sector budget squeeze + AI disruption + continued de-rating.\n  Rev CAGR FY27\u201330 = 4%; margin flat 27%; SBC 7% of rev; no net buyback.\n  IV = $235/sh [Estimate].\n\u2022 BASE (55%): Management executes 2030 plan within our conservative envelope.\n  Rev CAGR ~7.4%; margin to 29.2%; buyback \u22122.5%/yr. IV = $348/sh [Estimate].\n\u2022 BULL (25%): SaaS + transactions reaccelerate; AI/transactions become a new\n  revenue engine; revenue CAGR ~9\u201310% to $3.6B by 2030; EBITDA margin 31%;\n  EPS CAGR ~15%; exit 25x. IV = $440\u2013450/sh [Estimate].\nExpected value = 0.20\u00d7235 + 0.55\u00d7348 + 0.25\u00d7445 = $350/sh.\n50% CI: $290\u2013$430 | 80% CI: $235\u2013$520 [Assumption \u2014 scenario distribution].\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian update\nPrior (Section 1.5): P(\u2265base success) = 70%.\nEvidence: + (record SaaS bookings, raised guidance, raised 2030 targets) | \u2212 (ARR\ngrowth 8.2%, short interest rising, stock \u221239%).\nLikelihood ratio \u2248 1.1 \u2192 Posterior P(\u2265base) \u2248 75\u201380%; use 78%.\n\nStep 4-4. Expected price & MoS\nExpected price = $350 (EV). MoS = (348 \u2212 350.73)/348 = \u22120.8% \u2192 NONE.\n\nStep 4-5. Margin of safety thresholds\nAt $300: MoS 14% (adequate \u2192 buy zone). At $270: MoS 23% (strong).\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: current price embeds ~12% EPS CAGR through FY2030 with a 25x\nexit multiple \u2014 i.e., a 4\u20135 year outlook. My horizon: 5 years. Spread \u2248 0.\nNo meaningful horizon arbitrage. A 3-year horizon would justify only ~$295 (at 22x\nFY29E EPS of $16.40), implying 16% downside if you demand a 5-year payback. This raises\nthe bar on entry price.\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nConviction score (15 max):\n1. Business quality: 2.5/3 (wide moat, Excellent cash, Adequate ROIC)\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 1/3 (fair value, 0% MoS)\n3. Macro alignment: 1.5/3 (neutral/mild headwind)\n4. Thesis clarity: 2.5/3 (KPIs measurable: ARR, adj. EBITDA margin, FCF margin, SBC)\n5. Execution traceability: 2/3 (guidance raised; FTR/converts untested)\nTotal: 9.5/15 \u2192 MEDIUM. After Red-Team gap (Section 9): downgrade to 7.5/15 \u2192\nLOW-MEDIUM.\nConviction decay: \u22121 every 6 months absent confirming evidence (auto-applied next review).\nSize max: 3\u20134% portfolio weight (half of even the Low-Medium band, because MoS \u2248 0).\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Short interest: 9.94% of float (3.50M shares), DTC 4.37, MoM +2.5 pts [Actual \u2