t.\n 6. The Kaskela investigation and a ransomware/data-breach lawsuit create\n    overhangs [Unconfirmed].\n 7. Software sector de-rating on AI-cannibalization is NOT finished: the\n    sector's multiple resets tend to undershoot, and Tyler is still 26.8x\n    forward.\n9-2. Logic combat:\n - Long argument strength: 7/10\n - Short argument strength: 6/10\n Gap = 1 (< 2) \u2192 framework requires conviction downgrade of \u22120.5 steps.\n APPLIED: final conviction 11.5/15.\n The shorts' strongest point: the GAAP wedge and the deceleration. My\n honest reply: both are real, and both are partially in the price (the\n stock fell 39%; it now trades at 26.8x forward non-GAAP vs a 3-year\n average closer to 35x [Estimate]).\n9-3. Adversarial data: no Hindenburg-style report found for TYL.\n The Kaskela investigation and ransomware lawsuit [Unconfirmed] are the\n only named adversarial items; I could not verify them and did not\n materially adjust the valuation for them. If either produces a material\n settlement (>$200M), reduce fair value by ~$5/share [Arithmetic].\n9-4. Killed arguments:\n - \"AI kills Tyler\": only if AI replaces the need for workflow-specific\n   government software AND Tyler fails to embed AI. Monitor: AI-product\n   announcements and renewal rates. Not validated today.\n - \"The buyback is bad capital allocation\": it is value-accretive only if\n   the shares are below IV. At $350 vs IV $425, it is rational. Killed for\n   now, re-examine below $300 (then it is more accretive, not less).\n - \"Microsoft/Oracle will crush them\": there is no evidence of share loss\n   in the filed numbers. Killed until evidence appears.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500