u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n ACTION: BUY / ACCUMULATE (not \"load the boat\").\n\n Conviction: 11.5/15 [HIGH]\n Intrinsic value (base): $425 [Estimate] | MoS 17.5% [ADEQUATE]\n Scenario-weighted EV: $406 | 80% CI: $230\u2013545\n\n TARGET WEIGHT: 5% of portfolio, considered the maximum for this position\n given: (1) adequate-not-strong MoS; (2) crowding score 9/15; (3) macro\n rate risk; (4) red-team logic gap = 1.0.\n\n ENTRY STRATEGY (tranches):\n  - Tranche 1: 2.0% now at $350.73\n  - Tranche 2: +1.5% at $320\u2013330 (\u2248 24% MoS) OR after Q3'26 earnings\n    confirms FY26 guidance with revenue \u2265 $640M\n  - Tranche 3: +1.5% at $290\u2013300 (\u2248 30% MoS, strong) OR after the 10Y\n    falls below 4.2%\n  - Hard cap: 5%, never exceed\n\n EXIT / REDUCTION TRIGGERS:\n  - Sell 1/3 if recurring revenue growth < 6% for two consecutive quarters\n  - Sell entirely if owner FCF margin < 19% or GAAP operating margin < 14%\n  - Reduce 1/3 if 10Y > 5.5%\n  - Re-evaluate if legal items (Kaskela/ransomware) become material\n\n EXPECTED 3\u20135-YEAR OUTCOME (probability-weighted):\n  Price target ~$500\u2013570 in 3\u20135 years [Estimate based on \u00a74 base IV $425\n  compounding at ~10-13% EPS growth; bull $545 today's dollars]. The\n  investment works if the WACC gap closes; it fails if the market is right\n  to demand 9.4% (i.e., if the US enters a sustained high-inflation, high-\n  rate regime that compresses all long-duration assets).\n\n FINAL OPPOSITION (one-sentence bear retort): \"You are catching a falling\n  knife at 42x GAAP earnings with a decelerating 8% grower in a sector\n  that is being re-rated for AI disruption.\"\n MY ONE-SENTENCE REPLY: \"The knife has already fallen 39%; I am paying a\n  bottom-decile multiple of 5.8x sales and 26.8x forward non-GAAP earnings\n  for 87% recurring, budget-mandatory, government-locked revenue that the\n  market is discounting at 9.4% \u2014 and my position cap is sized so that if\n  I am wrong about rates, I lose at most ~1.7% of the portfolio.\"\n\nEND OF REPORT \u2014 DATA SOURCES VERIFIED AS OF 2026-08-26\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```"}, {"sample": 3, "iv": 285.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 362, "report": "```text\n================================================================================\nRS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS \u2014 TYLER TECHNOLOGIES, INC. (TYL)\nT0 Reference: 2026-08-24 17:59 | Price $350.73 | MCap $14.356B [Aggregator]\nPrepared: 2026-08-24 | Analysis Horizon: 3\u20135 years, DCF to 2034\n================================================================================\n\nSECTION 0 \u2014 ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTicker: TYL | Sector: Technology | Industry: Software-Application\nEnterprise Archetype: B - Quality Compounder (transitioning from legacy license to\nrecurring SaaS; ~85-90% of ERP clients on current version, courts legacy down to\n7% of clients from 89% three years ago [Actual \u2013 Q2 2026 call, Motley Fool\ntranscript]). Valuation Engine: ENGINE 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples) with\nowner-earnings (SBC-treated) FCF as the primary cash-flow definition, cross-checked\nagainst forward non-GAAP P/E and reverse DCF.\n\nReason for Engine Selection:\n1. Recurring revenue mix (SaaS +21.7% YoY in Q2 2026 [Actual], management-defined\n   FCF margin guided 26\u201328% FY2026 [Actual]) gives the earnings visibility required\n   for a 10-year explicit DCF.\n2. High switching costs (12\u201324-month implementation cycles, mission-critical\n   government workflows [Estimate \u2013 industry brief]) support stable terminal\n   assumptions.\n3. Multiples alone are unreliable here because GAAP vs non-GAAP earnings diverge by\n   ~76% (SBC $0.151B [Actual] plus amortization); only a cash-flow model exposes\n   that gap honestly.\n4. Base rates for platform transitions (~20\u201330% success [Framework base rate])\n   apply, but Tyler is already >60% through the transition; the DCF embeds the\n   remaining conversion as a revenue tailwind, not as speculative optionality.\n\nData Reliability Declaration: All financial statements are [Filed \u2013 SEC companyfacts]\nand outrank vendor restatements and web-brief claims, per pack Section 0. The pack's\nfiled series shows NO unexplained discontinuities requiring the Philip-Morris-style\nbasis-change adjustment (revenue/FCF series are smooth). The vendor's P/E (41.7x) is\ncomputed on TTM EPS $8.41 which does not reconcile to filed TTM GAAP EPS $7.42\n(0.325/0.043812 = $7.42 [Arithmetic]); I use filed figures throughout and flag the\ndiscrepancy as unresolved. The research brief's FY2026 EPS figures ($12.95\u201313.20)\nare NON-GAAP [Cross-checked \u2013 Zacks.com Q2 2026 earnings summary], not GAAP \u2014 the\napparent conflict with filed quarterly net income is a definitional one, resolved\nbelow.\n\nKey conclusion in one line: At $350.73 the stock prices in Tyler 2030 being fully\ndelivered; the margin of safety is negative (-7.9% to -11% depending on share\ncount), and the risk/reward is unattractive for new money.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1-1 Macro Variables (as of T0, retrieved 2026-08-24):\n- 10-Year Treasury: 4.71% [Actual \u2013 TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21; +0.04pt\n  1-month, +0.44pt YoY]\n- Real rates: ~2.0% [Estimate \u2013 10Y nominal less core CPI trending ~2.7-3.0%\n  [Unconfirmed]; Fed's July 2026 MPR states inflation above the 2% objective\n  [Actual \u2013 IronFX summary]]\n- Dollar (DXY): not obtainable in research budget [Unconfirmed]; treated as\n  neutral (TYL is ~99% domestic).\n- Fed stance: NEUTRAL TO TIGHTENING \u2014 June 2026 repriced toward a possible hike\n  (54% odds at one point [Estimate \u2013 interactivecrypto.com, 2026-06-08]);\n  no cuts priced near term.\n- Economic cycle position: LATE EXPANSION [Estimate \u2013 rate path, full employment,\n  above-target inflation]\n- Credit spreads: not retrievable in budget [Unconfirmed]; assumed normal/tight\n  given equity drawdowns without credit stress.\n- Liquidity: tightening bias as the Fed holds above neutral [Estimate].\n\nStep 1-2 Regime Probability Distribution:\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 30%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 30%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 25%\nTotal: 100%. Single-regime selection is prohibited; this distribution reflects\na late-cycle economy with a hawkish Fed and a 39% drawdown in TYL from its 52-week\nhigh \u2014 the market is pricing some stagflation/recession risk.\n\nStep 1-3 Macro Sensitivity (each -3 to +3):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: -1 (long-duration cash flows, but recurring\n  mission-critical revenue is rate-insensitive; de-rating risk dominates)\n- Dollar sensitivity: -0.5 (minimal international exposure; ~$1.6M ARR Australia\n  deal [Actual \u2013 Q2 call] is immaterial)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: -1.5 (software multiple compression in a liquidity\n  drawdown; the 2026 drawdown is itself evidence)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: -1 (local government budgets are countercyclical\n  but not immune; Texas contract decline is a reminder of program-specific risk)\n- Commodity sensitivity: -0.5 (negligible direct input)\nWeighted regime \u00d7 sensitivity: slightly negative. Macro Alignment: HEADWIND\n(-0.5 to conviction; strengthens split-entry discipline).\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1.5-1 Base Rates:\n- 5-year survival of a profitable, diversified public software company: ~95%\n  [Estimate \u2013 Framework base-rate table]\n- Probability a scaled enterprise-software company hits its 5-year plan: ~40\u201360%\n  [Estimate]\n- Platform/SaaS-transition success (as defined by >50% recurring mix + margin\n  expansion): ~60\u201370% when already >50% converted [Estimate; Tyler is ~85-90%\n  version-current by ERP clients]\n- Large-cap software maintaining 10%+ revenue growth for 5 years: ~30\u201340%\n  [Estimate \u2013 base rate of reversion toward 6\u20138% for a $2.3B business]\n- M&A of the FTR size (EV ~$258M [Unconfirmed \u2013 Intellectia vs FTR PR vary\n  $212.5M\u2013$258M]) achieving ROIC > WACC: ~35\u201345% [Estimate]\n\nStep 1.5-2 Deviation from Base Rates:\nMy base case assumes FY2026\u2013FY2030 revenue CAGR ~8.8% (2.555\u21923.587) \u2014 below\ntrailing 15.9% [Actual] and consistent with base-rate reversion. The deviation\nUPWARD from the 6\u20138% large-cap base rate is justified by: (a) SaaS ARR growth\nacceleration - 21.7% SaaS revenue growth [Actual]; (b) 85\u201390% of ERP clients on\nthe current version, implying a multi-year upgrade/cloud cycle [Actual]; (c)\nrecurring transactions (MS, titling, AP automation) creating a new annuity layer\n[Actual \u2013 Q2 call, 40+ AP automation wins, $10M titling ARR]. Insufficient to\njustify >2x base-rate, so no red-flag is triggered.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2-1 Business Understanding (3 sentences):\nTyler is the largest pure-play U.S. public-sector software provider, selling\nmission-critical ERP, courts/justice, public-safety, and payments systems to state\nand local governments. It is converting its installed base from on-premise licenses\nto cloud SaaS (SaaS revenue +21.7% YoY in Q2 2026 [Actual]) and layering\ntransaction-based revenue (payments, digital titling, AP automation) and AI\nassistants onto that base. Its economics rest on a 30-year installed base of\ngovernment agencies that cannot switch without disrupting core operations.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2 Moat Assessment (0-3 each):\n- Intangible assets (brand, gov-track record): 3\n- Switching costs (12-24 month implementation): 3\n- Network effects: 1 (ecosystem integrations, but not classic network effects)\n- Cost advantage: 2 (scale in niche verticals)\n- Economies of scale: 3 (R&D $245M guided FY2026 [Actual \u2013 Zacks] spread over\n  $2.5B+ revenue)\n- Regulatory barriers (gov procurement, security compliance): 3\n- Data moat (decades of gov workflows): 2\nTotal: 17/21 \u2014 WIDE MOAT [Estimate, scored from pack + research brief].\n\nMoat direction: STABLE TO WIDENING \u2014 court legacy down to 7% of clients (from\n89% three years ago [Actual]) increases per-customer module attach and switching\ncosts; competition from Oracle/MSFT is credible but largely lift-and-shift vs\nTyler's pure SaaS [Actual \u2013 Q2 call commentary]. Narrowing risk exists in AI-agent\ninterfaces reducing workflow-specific lock-in (see Red Team).\n\nStep 2-3 Lynch Classification:\nQuality Compounder (Stalwart/Fast-Grower hybrid): revenue CAGR 15.9% T5 [Actual],\nbut decelerating to ~9.5% guided FY2026 [Actual]; a 10% grower with 26\u201328% FCF\nmargin [Actual guidance]. Core checkpoints: growth durability (market share ~11.5%-\n15% of a fragmented market [Estimate \u2013 research brief]), retention (not disclosed\n[Unconfirmed]); management credibility (raised FY2026 guidance [Actual \u2013 Q2 call]).\n\nStep 2-4 Financial Strength Scorecard:\n- Revenue growth: FY2025 9.0% [Actual]; TTM 2026 7.9% [Arithmetic]; FY2026E 9.5%\n  [Actual guidance]\n- Operating margin: FY2025 15.3% [Actual]; 12-yr median 15.4% [Actual];\n  FY2026E +~100bps [Actual \u2013 Q2 call]\n- EPS growth: FY2026E non-GAAP +17.2% [Estimate \u2013 consensus; pack Section 8]\n- ROE: 9.73% [Aggregator]; GAAP net margin 13.6% [Arithmetic];\n  ROIC GAAP ~7.5% [Estimate, computed below]\n- FCF margin: 27.4% filed basis; 26.6% broader capex basis; guided 26\u201328%\n  [Actual]\n- SBC: 6.5% of revenue FY2025 [Arithmetic] \u2014 material\n- Net debt: pre-convert ~-$0.48B net CASH [Arithmetic]; post-convert ~$0.75B net\n  DEBT [Estimate, see Section 3]\nJudgment: GOOD financials, but GAAP quality is mediocre (net income / OCF gap of\n-$0.406B TTM [Arithmetic] is driven primarily by SBC and WC swings; Piotroski 8/9\n[Arithmetic] and accruals ratio -0.0625 [Arithmetic] indicate clean accruals).\n\nStep 2-5 Capital Allocation Scorecard (0-3 each, total 15):\n1. Buyback efficiency: 3 \u2014 >5.5% of shares retired YTD 2026 [Actual \u2013 Q2 call],\n   and prior-year repurchases at lower prices (calculated avg ~$330 vs 10-yr avg\n   P/E below today's \u2014 see Section 3). Aggressive below-average multiple.\n2. M&A track record: 2 \u2014 FTR ($212.5\u2013258M [Unconfirmed] for ~$44M annualized\n   revenue contribution, i.e. ~5-6x trailing revenue [Estimate]) is sensible, but\n   the 2021 acquisitions created goodwill; no 5-yr impairment analysis available\n   [Unconfirmed].\n3. Capex efficiency: 3 \u2014 capex only $16M filed / $33M vendor [Actual] in a\n   $2.43B revenue business; incremental ROIC on cloud transition high.\n4. Mix rationality: 2 \u2014 converting, debt-funded buybacks; issuing $1.25B+ of\n   converts (0.5% coupon, due 2031 [Actual \u2013 SEC PR, 05/12/2026]) to fund\n   buybacks is leveraged, but the coupon is so low it is close to risk-free\n   carry. Deduct 1 for the leverage.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3 \u2014 FY2026 EPS guide raised, revenue raised at Q1 [Actual \u2013\n   investing.com], Q2 beat on record bookings [Actual].\nTotal: 13/15. Insider ownership 0.52% [Aggregator] < 1%: no bonus. No insider\nbuying; 2026 insider selling noted by research brief [Unconfirmed]. No penalty\napplied absent 6-month data.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2.5-1 R&D Capitalization:\nFY2026 R&D guide: $245M [Actual \u2013 Zacks.com]. FY2025 R&D estimated $235M\n[Assumption \u2013 interpolated]. 5-year straight-line amortization:\n- FY2025 adjusted EBIT = 0.358 + 0.235 - 0.047 = $0.546B [Arithmetic/Estimate]\n- Adjusted EBIT margin = 23.4% vs GAAP 15.3% [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 2.5-2 SBC Treatment (critical):\n- Adjusted FCF = reported FCF - SBC = 0.638 - 0.151 = $0.487B FY2025 [Arithmetic]\n- Adjusted FCF margin = 20.9% [Arithmetic]\n- The company's guided 26\u201328% FCF margin does NOT subtract SBC [Estimate \u2014\n  definition implied]; this is the single biggest quality-of-earnings adjustment\n  in the model.\n\nStep 2.5-3 Operating Leases:\n- Operating leases $43M FY2025 [Actual]; capitalize at 7x = ~$0.30B debt-equivalent\n  [Assumption]; adjusted net debt rises, but immaterial to IV (<1%).\n\nStep 2.5-4 Brand/Data:\n- Not quantified [Unconfirmed \u2014 no Interbrand-style valuation retrieved]\n\nStep 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC:\n- GAAP NOPAT = 0.316 \u00d7 (1 - 0.22 [Assumption]) = $0.246B; Invested capital =\n  equity 3.703 + debt 0.600 - cash 1.015 = $3.288B [Arithmetic] \u2192 GAAP ROIC = 7.5%\n  [Arithmetic]\n- Adjusted: NOPAT $0.426B + unamortized R&D ~$0.70B [Estimate] = ~$4.0B \u2192 10.6%\n  [Estimate]\n- Gap of 3.1pp is explained by goodwill-heavy balance sheet; still below an 8.0%\n  WACC. This is a business whose returns on TANGIBLE capital are stellar but whose\n  total capital returns are mediocre because of acquisition accounting.\n\nStep 2.5-6 Owner Earnings (Buffett):\nOE = NI 0.316 + D&A 0.138 - maintenance capex ~0.033 [Estimate \u2013 vendor basis] -\n\u0394WC -0.287 [Arithmetic; swings wildly, negative in FY2025] = ~0.316 + 0.138 -\n0.033 + 0.287 = $0.708B before SBC; minus SBC 0.151 = $0.557B [Estimate].\nCross-check: my DCF's FY2026 starting owner-FCF is $0.524B; the series is\nconsistent within ~5%.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION:\n- Risk-free rate: 4.71% [Actual \u2013 10Y UST, TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21]\n- ERP: 4.5% [Assumption; standard long-run estimate, not retrieved]\n- Beta: 0.85 [Assumption; vendor beta is 0.809 [Aggregator \u2013 window unknown];\n  I use 0.85 to reflect the 2026 drawdown]\n- Cost of equity = 4.71 + 0.85 \u00d7 4.5 = 8.54%\n- Cost of debt (pre-tax) = 5.5% [Assumption; converts carry 0.5% but effective\n  market yield higher; senior debt yield unretrieved]\n- Debt weight: ~11.4% (debt $2.04B [Estimate] / EV $17.9B [Arithmetic])\n- WACC = 0.886 \u00d7 8.54 + 0.114 \u00d7 5.5 \u00d7 (1 - 0.22) = 7.57 + 0.49 = 8.06 \u2192 8.0%\n  [Assumption; rounded]\n\nREVENUE FORECAST (each driver sourced):\n| Year | Revenue ($B) | Growth | Source |\n|------|-------------|--------|--------|\n| 2025A| 2.332        | +9.0%  | [Actual \u2013 filed] |\n| 2026E| 2.555        | +9.5%  | [Actual \u2013 guidance mid 2.535-2.575, Zacks] |\n| 2027E| 2.808        | +9.9%  | [Estimate \u2013 consensus revenue growth 0.0991, pack] |\n| 2028E| 3.061        | +9.0%  | [Assumption; ex-Texas underlying ~11.3% [Actual guide] fades; 2030 target ~10% CAGR [Actual \u2013 Morningstar headline]] |\n| 2029E| 3.321        | +8.5%  | [Assumption, same basis] |\n| 2030E| 3.587        | +8.0%  | [Assumption, same basis] |\n| 2031E| 3.838        | +7.0%  | [Assumption \u2013 base-rate reversion] |\n| 2032E| 4.068        | +6.0%  | [Assumption \u2013 base-rate reversion] |\n| 2033E| 4.271        | +5.0%  | [Assumption \u2013 base-rate reversion] |\n| 2034E| 4.442        | +4.0%  | [Assumption \u2013 fade to terminal] |\n\nNote: The path beyond FY2027 is NOT established by any retrieved guidance. The\nTyler 2030 goal is \"~10% top-line growth and substantial margin expansion\"\n[Actual \u2013 Morningstar report headline, 2026-06-09], but management cautioned\n\"growth and margin expansion will not be linear\" [Estimate \u2013 TIKR blog].\nMy post-2027 path is an assumption that lands 2030 revenue at $3.59B, ~7% below\na straight 10% CAGR from 2025 ($3.75B). I believe this is appropriately\nconservative; the bull case pays up for the full 2030 plan.\n\nOWNER-FCF MARGIN (post-SBC) FORECAST:\n| Year | Margin | Source |\n|------|--------|--------|\n| 2025A| 20.9%  | [Arithmetic: (0.638-0.151)/2.332] |\n| 2026E| 20.5%  | [Estimate: mgmt-guided 26-28% [Actual] minus SBC ~6.5% [Estimate]] |\n| 2027E| 21.0%  | [Assumption: op margin +100bps guided, SBC roughly flat] |\n| 2028E| 21.5%  | [Assumption: cloud mix + pricing] |\n| 2029E| 22.0%  | [Assumption: AI-driven efficiency, labor-augmentation pricing] |\n| 2030E| 22.5%  | [Assumption: \"substantial margin expansion\" [Actual] but SBC still weighs] |\n| 2031-34E| 23.0% | [Assumption: plateau] |\n\nOwner-FCF = Revenue \u00d7 margin. This embeds capex ($33M broader definition\n[Estimate \u2013 vendor]) and \u0394WC near zero (the 11-year arithmetic average of \u0394WC is\n-0.015B [Arithmetic]). Capex is a rounding error in this model; the dominant\ndrivers are revenue growth and SBC-adjusted margin. Explicit per-year cash flows\n($B): 2026: 0.524 | 2027: 0.590 | 2028: 0.658 | 2029: 0.731 | 2030: 0.807 |\n2031: 0.883 | 2032: 0.936 | 2033: 0.982 | 2034: 1.022.\n\nTERMINAL VALUE:\n- Terminal growth: 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 below nominal GDP, consistent with\n  long-run public-sector IT spend growth]\n- TV2034 = 1.022 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.08 - 0.03) = $21.06B [Arithmetic]\n- PV(TV) at 8.0% end-year over 10 years = 21.06 \u00d7 0.4632 = $9.76B\n\nNET DEBT (reconciled):\n- Cash (FY2025): $1.015B [Actual] + short-term investments $0.082B [Actual]\n- + Convert proceeds $1.4375B [Unconfirmed \u2014 K&L Gates title references\n  $1.4375B incl. greenshoe; the SEC PR [Actual] says $1.25B priced upsized]\n- - YTD buybacks (>5.5% \u00d7 40.93M \u00d7 ~$360 avg \u2248 $0.81B [Estimate])\n- - FTR acquisition ~$0.26B [Unconfirmed]\n- = Cash/investments \u2248 $1.48B [Estimate]\n- Debt: LT debt $0.600B [Actual] + converts $1.4375B [Unconfirmed] + capitalised\n  operating leases $0.30B [Assumption] = $2.34B\n- NET DEBT = $0.86B [Estimate; rounds to $0.9B for conservatism].\nCross-check: vendor's $0.643B total-debt figure [Aggregator] is pre-convert; the\ndiscrepancy is explained, not a red flag. Pack warning respected: the two\nlong-term debt tags are NOT summed; I used the noncurrent tag only.\n\nDCF RESULT (BASE CASE):\n- PV of explicit FCFs (2026\u20132034): $4.74B [Arithmetic]\n- PV of TV: $9.76B \u2192 EV = $14.50B\n- Equity = 14.50 - 0.90 = $13.60B\n- Fully diluted shares: 42.0M [Assumption: 40.93M outstanding [Actual] + ~1.0M\n  net future SBC dilution over 4 years, partially offset by announced buybacks;\n  the $1.4375B converts are treated as DEBT at 0.5% coupon because they are\n  deeply out of the money at $350.73; if the stock\u2019s bull case converts them,\n  dilution of ~3.6M shares is offset by removal of the liability \u2014 I model the\n  bearish treatment]\n- INTRINSIC VALUE BASE = $13.60 / 0.042 = $324/share [Estimate]\n- 50% CI: $290\u2013$370 [Assumption] | 80% CI: $230\u2013$470 [Assumption]\n\nTORNADO SENSITIVITY (\u00b11\u03c3 on key drivers):\n| Driver | -1\u03c3 | +1\u03c3 | Delta |\n|--------|-----|-----|-------|\n| WACC (8.0% \u00b1 0.5pp) | $285 | $375 | \u00b114% |\n| Terminal growth (3.0% \u00b1 0.5pp) | $300 | $350 | \u00b18% |\n| 2034 FCF margin (23% \u00b1 1pp) | $300 | $350 | \u00b18% |\n| 2030 revenue (3.59 \u00b1 0.3B) | $300 | $350 | \u00b18% |\nTop 3 thesis KPIs: (1) owner-FCF margin, (2) revenue CAGR 2026\u201330, (3) WACC /\n10-year rate.\n\nMULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK (Engine discipline \u2014 no DCF-only conclusion):\n- FY2026 non-GAAP EPS: $12.95\u201313.20 [Actual guidance] \u2192 fwd P/E = 26.6\u201327.1x\n  [Arithmetic]\n- Fair P/E for a 10% grower with 26% FCF margins at 4.71% 10Y: 22\u201325x\n  [Assumption \u2014 sourced from a standard P/E/growth/rate framework, not\n  retrieved] \u2192 fair value $285\u2013330\n- GAAP TTM EPS $7.42 [Arithmetic] \u2192 trailing GAAP P/E 47.3x; fair 35\u201340x \u2192\n  $260\u2013297\n- Reverse DCF at $350.73: EV = ($350.73 \u00d7 0.042) + 0.90 = $15.63B. Required\n  owner-FCF 2034 = 15.63 / (PV exit factor 0.4632 \u00d7 (1/(r-g)) + PV flow factor)\n  \u2248 $1.28B organic \u2014 i.e. the market requires the bull case: 9.5%+ growth through\n  2034 AND a 25% owner-FCF margin. Feasible, but a high bar.\nMultiples verdict: consistent with DCF \u2014 fair value $285\u2013330, midpoint ~$308;\nmy DCF base $324 is at the top of that range because the model gives credit for\nthe cloud transition the market has already partly de-rated.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 3.5-1 Competitive Response: (-1) If Tyler succeeds with AI/transaction\nrevenue, Oracle and Microsoft Gov Cloud will respond with subsidized bundles.\nImpact is moderate because procurement cycles are slow and Tyler\u2019s data incumbency\nis protected by 30 years of migrations; but price pressure in mid-sized counties\n(where Tyler is 30\u201360% more expensive per research brief [Estimate]) could\nintensify.\n\nStep 3.5-2 Reflexivity: MILD POSITIVE today. The 39% drawdown [Arithmetic on\n52-wk range] reduces SBC attractiveness and the acquisition currency; but the\ncompany\u2019s >5.5% buyback at lower prices is a positive reflexive signal of\ninsider belief. A falling stock does NOT endanger Tyler\u2019s operations (no debt\ncovenant risk, mission-critical government contracts are sticky) \u2014 negative\nreflexivity is lower than at typical high-beta software.\n\nStep 3.5-3 Customer Evolution: The early adopter segment is done. The mainstream\nis now converting: ERP version currency 85\u201390% [Actual] and court legacy down to\n7% [Actual]. As penetration grows, LTV/CAC will worsen slightly (new logos are\nsmaller, mid-sized deals \u2014 \u201chigh volume of mid-sized deals rather than large\nmega contracts\u201d [Actual]) and churn could rise at the low end. This is already\nembedded in my assumption that growth fades to 4% by 2034.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRIOR (Layer 1.5): Large-cap software achieves ~10% growth + margin expansion\nfor 5 years with ~40\u201360% probability [Estimate]. Tyler\u2019s specific transition\nstatus (85\u201390% version current) updates the prior upward.\n\nPOSTERIOR UPDATE (evidence since 2025: raised FY2026 guidance [Actual], record\nSaaS bookings [Actual], 40+ AP wins [Actual], AI revenue ramp pushed to 2H2027\n[Actual \u2014 Q2 call], FTR closed [Actual]):\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Revenue 2030 | Owner-FCF margin 2034 | WACC | IV/share |\n|----------|------|--------------|------------------------|------|----------|\n| Disaster: breach/regulatory/AI disruption; budgets freeze | 5% | $2.8B | 16% | 8.5% | $120 |\n| Bear: growth 5%, margins flat, 10Y >5% | 20% | $3.25B | 20% | 8.5% | $205 |\n| Base: 8.8% CAGR 26\u201330, margin 23% | 55% | $3.59B | 23% | 8.0% | $324 |\n| Bull: 11% CAGR, AI/transactions accelerate, SBC fades | 20% | $4.30B | 25% | 7.75% | $525 |\nTotal: 100%. Base is the single highest probability. Constraints respected: no\nsingle-point, no rounding off of 5% tail risk.\n\nEXPECTED PRICE = 0.05\u00d7120 + 0.20\u00d7205 + 0.55\u00d7324 + 0.20\u00d7525 = $331 [Arithmetic]\nMARGIN OF SAFETY (base) = (324 - 350.73)/324 = -8.3% \u2192 NONE.\nJudgment: Limited to None. The price is 8% above base IV and the expected value\nis 6% below price. A value investor waits.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMarket-implied horizon: at $350.73, the market pays 27x forward non-GAAP EPS on\na 9.5% grower \u2014 that implies ~10 years of growth continuation at the guided\ntrajectory and a 25% exit FCF margin [Arithmetic/Estimate]. The market is\nvaluing the FULL Tyler 2030 plan without haircut. My horizon (3\u20135 years) sees\nrevenue $2.8\u20133.6B and FCF $0.59\u20130.81B \u2014 the equity value under MY 5-year view is\nEV = PV(5 yrs FCF) + PV(terminal at conservative margins) \u2248 0.485+0.506+0.522+\n0.537+0.549 + (0.807\u00d71.03/(0.08-0.03) \u00d7 0.6806) = 2.60 + 11.32 = $13.9B \u2192 ~$310\u2013330\nper share [Estimate]. Spread = My horizon value minus market value \u2248 -$20 to -$40\nper share. NO horizon arbitrage exists at T0; the market\u2019s error is not in\nhorizon but in margin (it is paying for the bull case).\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 5-1 Conviction Score (0-15):\n- Business quality: 3/3 (wide moat, 21.7% SaaS growth, 26\u201328% FCF margin guidance)\n- Valuation attractiveness: 1/3 (MoS -8.3%; fwd P/E 27x non-GAAP,