Conviction downgrade of 1 point (already applied in Section 5).\n\nStep 9-3 ADVERSARIAL DATA: Hindenburg has not published on TYL [Unconfirmed];\nKaskela Law investigation is a standard post-drop shareholder-shop solicitation,\nnot an SEC action [Unconfirmed]. No confirmed regulatory proceeding.\n\nStep 9-4 KILLED ARGUMENTS:\n- \u201cThe 46% drawdown means it\u2019s cheap.\u201d KILLED: the drawdown is a de-rating from\n  an unsustainable $578, not a decline below value. The current price equals the\n  DCF fair value at bull-ish execution.\n- \u201cConvertibles are always dilution.\u201d KILLED: at 0.5% coupon with capped calls\n  and a conversion price likely above $400 [Unconfirmed \u2014 cap price NOT\n  retrieved], the converts are cheap debt in the base case; they become equity\n  only if shareholders win.\n- \u201cFTR acquisition indicates empire building.\u201d KILLED: $11M Q2 revenue on a\n  ~$258M EV [Unconfirmed] is ~5-6x trailing, reasonable for court software with\n  international reach. REVIVAL CONDITION: if integration costs exceed guidance\n  and FTR\u2019s $11M quarterly run-rate does not grow, revisit.\n- \u201cThe Texas payments decline is a failure.\u201d KILLED: the company guided ex-\n  Texas growth at 11.3% [Actual]; the Texas contract was a low-margin,\n  program-specific revenue, now cycling off.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 10 \u2014 AUDIT RESULTS (v2.0 expanded)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA. Methodology: Engine 1 correctly selected (quality compounder, recurring\n   revenue). Blended engine NOT required (transition is >80% complete by ERP\n   version currency [Actual]). PASS.\nB. Valuation: DCF uses explicit 10-yr forecasts, sourced drivers, mid-case\n   terminal growth 3.0% < nominal GDP, WACC 8.0% derived transparently.\n   No DCF-only conclusion; multiples and reverse DCF agree within \u00b115%. PASS.\nC. Bias: Overconfidence check \u2014 the bull case is a 20% probability, below the\n   consensus target (mean $426 implies 32x forward [Arithmetic]), and my IV is\n   24% below the sell-side mean \u2014 I am not anchored to consensus. The 5%\n   disaster scenario and CVaR -66% are explicitly carried. PASS.\nD. Consistency: The same SBC treatment is used in margins, FCF, and share\n   counts; net debt includes the convert in both EV and share-count assumptions\n   (conservative double-count). No time-point mixing: T0 is 2026-08-24; T-1 is\n   FY2025; Tpf is Q2 2026 (filed 2026-07-29) \u2014 explicitly distinguished. PASS.\nE. Narrative: All qualitative claims (moat, AI, competitive) are tagged and\n   cross-checked to the Q2 call or research brief. No TAM narrative used in the\n   DCF. PASS.\nF. Red Team: Section 9 completed; logic gap 1.0 applied. PASS.\nG. Data Integrity: (1) Vendor P/E (41.7x) does not reconcile to filed TTM EPS\n   ($7.42) \u2014 flagged as unresolved, I used filed data. (2) Vendor total debt\n   $0.643B excludes the new converts \u2014 flagged, I added them. (3) Capex\n   definition mismatch ($0.016B filed vs $0.033B vendor) \u2014 I used the broader\n   definition in margin assumptions. (4) FTR purchase price discrepancy\n   ($212.5M vs $258M) \u2014 marked [Unconfirmed] and treated as immaterial\n   (\u00b10.3% of IV). (5) The pack warning about tag changes was checked:\n   the revenue/FCF series is smooth, no basis-change artifacts. PASS with\n   flagged items.\n\n================================================================================\nSECTION 11 \u2014 DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDate: 2026-08-24 | T0 Price: $350.73 | MCap: $14.356B\n- IV Base: $324 | E[V]: $331 | 50% CI: $290\u2013370 | 80% CI: $230\u2013470\n- MoS: -8.3% | Conviction: MEDIUM (8.5/15)\n- Key assumptions: FY26 rev $2.555B (guided); 26-30 revenue CAGR 8.8%;\n  owner-FCF margin to 23% by 2030; WACC 8.0%; ter